 "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "1cfef4f8-dbd4-422b-a6ff-26b1d9f0efe3", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790218576.040819, "updated_at": 1790218660.518357, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yes. **Keep MiMo. Change the seat, not the model.** That is actually a useful test of the doctrine: MiMo just performed the Luce verification pass; now you reload it under the **Nico bootstrap** and explicitly hand it an implementation contract. **MODEL != SEAT.**\n\nAnd Luce gave us enough. The key finding is that the Command Center already exists and is substantially implemented; it simply is **not running**, while the live OpenCode/Kimi terminals are operating outside it. fileciteturn1file0L5-L16 The minimum slice Luce identified is also clean: boot it, add a read-only process/session inventory, expose model/provider/CWD while leaving seat UNKNOWN when it cannot be proven, and fix the stale launcher. fileciteturn1file0L177-L189\n\nSo after MiMo loads the **Nico bootstrap**, give it this:\n\n> **GCE DEVPAD COMMAND CENTER — NICO IMPLEMENTATION SLICE**\n>\n> **Authority:** Don  \n> **Seat:** Nico  \n> **Runtime/model:** Runtime-declared — MODEL != SEAT  \n> **Upstream verification:** Luce ground-state reconciliation, 2026-09-23  \n> **Mode:** IMPLEMENT / VERIFY / MINIMUM SLICE  \n> **Primary target:** `GCE_DON\\command-center\\`\n>\n> ## Mission\n>\n> Luce has completed ground-state reconciliation.\n>\n> **Do not redesign DevPad.**\n>\n> The canonical GCE Command Center already exists at:\n>\n> `GCE_DON\\command-center\\`\n>\n> It is currently down. The immediate objective is to turn the existing implementation into a usable primary operational view for Don with the **smallest safe implementation slice possible**.\n>\n> Desired outcome:\n>\n> **ONE VIEW → LIVE SESSIONS → MODEL/PROVIDER/PROJECT IDENTITY → SEAT WHEN VERIFIED → BASIC OPERATIONAL VISIBILITY**\n>\n> We are solving Don's terminal-sprawl problem, not building a new platform.\n>\n> ---\n>\n> ## Luce-established ground truth\n>\n> Treat these as the starting facts:\n>\n> - Existing Command Center: Node HTTP/WebSocket + xterm.js + node-pty + seat registry + adapters.\n> - Normal port: `4727`.\n> - Command Center is **not currently running**.\n> - Existing external runtime estate includes:\n>   - 3 OpenCode TUIs\n>   - 1 Kimi CLI\n>   - inbox MCP children\n>   - VoiceBridge\n> - Those existing TUIs were not launched by DevPad and therefore must **not** be assigned seats by inference.\n> - Current OpenCode session metadata exposes runtime/session information but does not reliably encode seat identity.\n> - `runtime-state.json` is presently absent.\n> - OS process/port inventory is presently missing from DevPad.\n> - Existing `Tools\\shortcuts\\Command Center.lnk` points to a stale/missing path.\n> - Historical BOM/config launch failure appears corrected in current code but must be re-tested rather than assumed fixed.\n>\n> ---\n>\n> # PHASE 0 — PRESERVE CURRENT WORK\n>\n> **Do not kill, restart, close, adopt, attach to, or mutate any existing OpenCode/Kimi terminal.**\n>\n> Existing work may be active.\n>\n> Do not rotate credentials.\n>\n> Do not inspect secret values.\n>\n> Do not touch unrelated projects.\n>\n> Do not modify ECQ Quant DevPad. It is a separate application.\n>\n> Do not refactor the Command Center architecture.\n>\n> Before edits, capture:\n>\n> - current git/repo status if applicable\n> - files you expect to touch\n> - current relevant PIDs\n> - current listeners on DevPad-related ports\n>\n> ---\n>\n> # PHASE 1 — PROVE THE EXISTING COMMAND CENTER\n>\n> Start with the implementation already on disk.\n>\n> 1. Inspect:\n>    - `server.js`\n>    - `start-devpad.cmd`\n>    - `config.json`\n>    - `seats.js`\n>    - `adapter.js`\n>    - relevant adapters\n>    - `public/index.html`\n>    - `public/renderer.js`\n>    - `public/styles.css`\n>\n> 2. Run existing syntax/build checks appropriate to this project.\n>\n> 3. Start the Command Center using its intended launcher.\n>\n> 4. Verify:\n>    - listener on `:4727`\n>    - UI loads\n>    - `/api/seats`\n>    - existing board renders\n>    - WebSocket path works\n>    - VoiceBridge status endpoint works\n>    - existing panes do not immediately crash\n>\n> 5. Re-test one safe seat-launch path sufficiently to determine whether the historical BOM/config issue is actually resolved.\n>\n> If existing code fails, **repair only the failure necessary to establish this baseline**.\n>\n> Do not proceed into broad cleanup.\n>\n> ---\n>\n> # PHASE 2 — ADD READ-ONLY LIVE PROCESS / SESSION INVENTORY\n>\n> Implement the smallest practical inventory surface inside the existing Command Center.\n>\n> Add a **read-only backend endpoint** that discovers relevant live processes.\n>\n> Minimum fields where observable:\n>\n> - PID\n> - process/executable\n> - parent PID\n> - command line\n> - working directory\n> - listening port(s)\n> - runtime type\n> - OpenCode/Kimi/session identifier if available\n> - model if directly observable\n> - provider if directly observable\n> - project/CWD if directly observable\n> - seat\n> - confidence/source\n>\n> Seat handling is binding:\n>\n> **If seat identity is not directly established, display `UNKNOWN`.**\n>\n> Never derive:\n>\n> `MiMo → Luce`\n>\n> `Kimi → Ragno`\n>\n> `GLM → Chiave`\n>\n> or any equivalent model-to-seat shortcut.\n>\n> **MODEL != SEAT.**\n>\n> Preserve that separation in both backend representation and UI.\n>\n> Prefer read-only OS/session inspection.\n>\n> Do not require elevated privileges unless absolutely necessary.\n>\n> If Windows refuses a process CWD, return:\n>\n> `UNKNOWN`\n>\n> rather than failing the entire inventory.\n>\n> ---\n>\n> # PHASE 3 — A