nconfig.json\n→ seats.js\n→ adapter.js\n→ adapters/opencode.js\n→ resolved launch spec\n→ spawned OpenCode command/process\n\nDetermine exactly:\n\nA. What model/provider values exist in config.\nB. Whether those fields survive resolveLaunchSpec.\nC. Whether they are passed to OpenCode at launch.\nD. Whether OpenCode supports model selection by:\n   - command-line flag\n   - environment variable\n   - per-project config\n   - session initialization\n   - another existing supported mechanism.\n\nDO NOT GUESS THE FLAG OR SYNTAX.\n\nInspect:\n- current OpenCode adapter\n- existing working launch commands\n- `opencode --help` / local CLI help\n- current OpenCode config\n- any prior DevPad implementation that successfully selected a non-default model.\n\nWe need observed local behavior, not documentation assumptions.\n\n==================================================\n3. IMPORTANT CONFIG RULE\n==================================================\n\nIf DevPad currently contains fields like:\n\nseat\nprovider\nmodel\ncwd\n\nbut the launcher only consumes seat/cwd, then report that explicitly.\n\nA JSON field that says:\n\nmodel: \"glm-5.1\"\n\ndoes NOT count as working unless the spawned OpenCode session actually starts on GLM 5.1.\n\nThe UI must reflect runtime truth, not desired config.\n\n==================================================\n4. REQUIRED SEAT/RUNTIME TARGETS\n==================================================\n\nPreserve the current corrected board.\n\nROW 1\n1 Coda\n2 Chiave\n3 Vittorio\n\nROW 2\n4 Nico\n5 VoiceBridge\n6 Luce\n\nROW 3\n7 Talon\n8 Keel\n9 Falcon\n\nBindings explicitly specified by Don:\n\nCoda\n→ Browser / ChatGPT\n→ persistent authenticated browser profile\n\nChiave\n→ OpenCode\n→ Chiave identity/bootstrap\n→ GLM 5.1\n\nVittorio\n→ Kimi native/subscription CLI\n→ Vittorio identity/bootstrap\n\nNico\n→ OpenCode\n→ Nico identity/bootstrap\n→ retain currently approved model unless another binding is already explicitly configured\n\nVoiceBridge\n→ existing VoiceBridge service\n\nLuce\n→ OpenCode\n→ Luce identity/bootstrap\n→ Big Pickle\n\nTalon\n→ OpenCode\n→ Talon identity/bootstrap\n→ use its configured/approved binding, but VERIFY actual runtime\n\nKeel\n→ OpenCode\n→ Keel identity/bootstrap\n→ use its configured/approved binding, but VERIFY actual runtime\n\nFalcon\n→ OpenCode\n→ Falcon identity/bootstrap\n→ use its configured/approved binding, but VERIFY actual runtime\n\nDo not assign new models to Nico/Talon/Keel/Falcon merely because Big Pickle is currently appearing.\n\nIf their desired model is not explicitly established in current authority/config, preserve UNKNOWN / CURRENT until Don decides.\n\n==================================================\n5. FIX OPENCode LAUNCH BINDING\n==================================================\n\nOnce you identify the actual supported local OpenCode model-selection mechanism:\n\nImplement the smallest change so a DevPad seat launch can explicitly request its configured provider/model.\n\nDesired conceptual contract:\n\nseat config:\n  seat identity\n  cwd\n  runtime = opencode\n  provider\n  model\n\nlaunch adapter:\n  validates runtime/provider/model\n  converts them into the ACTUAL OpenCode-supported invocation/config mechanism\n\nspawned process:\n  opens under seat identity CWD\n  uses requested model/provider\n\nDo not hardcode Chiave or Luce logic into server.js if the existing adapter/config system can express it generically.\n\nPrefer fixing the OpenCode adapter once.\n\n==================================================\n6. PROVE IT WITH TWO DIFFERENT MODELS\n==================================================\n\nThe regression is not fixed merely because OpenCode launches.\n\nProve model binding by launching at least TWO OpenCode seats with intentionally different bindings.\n\nMinimum proof:\n\nChiave\n→ OpenCode\n→ GLM 5.1\n\nLuce\n→ OpenCode\n→ Big Pickle\n\nThen inspect the actual running/session state and prove they are different models.\n\nThe pane label/config alone is insufficient evidence.\n\nIf OpenCode exposes session/model state, use it.\n\n==================================================\n7. PRESERVE WORKING IDENTITY BEHAVIOR\n==================================================\n\nDo not disturb the part that is working.\n\nCurrent identity-home behavior is good:\n\nGCE_IDENTITY\\Chiave\nGCE_IDENTITY\\Nico\nGCE_IDENTITY\\Luce\nGCE_IDENTITY\\Talon\nGCE_IDENTITY\\Keel\nGCE_IDENTITY\\Falcon\netc.\n\nThe correction must preserve:\n\ncorrect seat\n→ correct identity home\n→ correct bootstrap\n→ correct runtime\n→ correct model/provider\n\nThis is a four-layer binding.\n\nDo not solve model selection by flattening identity.\n\n==================================================\n8. KIMI IS THE REFERENCE IMPLEMENTATION\n==================================================\n\nThe previous Kimi setup is useful evidence.\n\nIt successfully demonstrated:\n\nseat\n→ correct identity home\n→ explicit runtime executable\n→ correct subscription/runtime behavior\n\nUse that launch architecture as a reference for how explicit runtime binding should work.\n\nDo not replace working native Kimi with OpenCode merely to make everything uniform.\n\nUniformity is NOT the goal.\n\nCorrect runtime per seat is the goal.\n\n==================================================\n9. DO NOT TOUCH\n==================================================\n\nDo not:\n- rebuild inventory\n- redesign the grid\n- change VoiceBridge\n- change numeric tile addressing\n- modify ECQ\n- alter seat doctrine\n- rewrite identities\n- convert all runtimes to OpenCode\n- rotate credentials\n- inspect secret values\n- invent model substitutions\n\n==================================================\n10. STOP CONDITIONS\n==================================================\n\nStop and return to Don if:\n\n- OpenCode cannot accept an explicit model at launch/session creation\n- GLM 5.1 is unavailable through the currently configured OpenCode providers\n- restoring native Kimi requires a login/subscription action from Don\n- a provider credential is missing\n- the desired model for Nico/Talon/Keel/Falcon is not explicitly established\n- correcting model selection requires broad architecture work\n\nDo not silently fall back to Big Pickle.\n\nA failed requested model must be visible as FAILED/BLOCKED, not replaced invisibly by the default model.\n\n==================================================\n11. REQUIRED RETURN\n==================================================\n\nRESULT:\nPASS / PARTIAL / BLOCKED\n\nROOT CAUSE:\nExplain exactly why all OpenCode panes defaulted to Big Pickle.\n\nVITTORIO:\nConfirm native Kimi restored or state blocker.\n\nOPENCode MODEL BINDING:\nExplain the actual supported local mechanism discovered.\n\nFILES CHANGED:\nExact files and purpose.\n\nPROOF:\nReturn actual observed runtime/model for:\n\nChiave\nLuce\nVittorio\n\nThen return current runtime/model for:\nNico\nTalon\nKeel\nFalcon\n\nDo not report config intent as runtime proof.\n\nFINAL VERDICT:\nOne of:\n\nDEVPAD RUNTIME BINDINGS CORRECT AND VERIFIED\n\nDEVPAD IDENTITIES CORRECT — MODEL BINDING PARTIAL\n\nKIMI RESTORED — OPENCode MODEL BINDING BLOCKED\n\nBLOCKED\n\nDo not proceed into additional DevPad features after this pass.\n```\n\nAnd I’d tell Nico one more thing outside the prompt: **don’t “fix” this by putting GLM or Kimi names in the tile headers.** We need the actual spawned runtime to prove it.\n\nThe good news is that this is narrower than it looks. **The hard part—seat → identity home—is working.** We need to repair the final hop:\n\n**identity → launcher → provider/model.**", "text_chars": 10285, "code_blocks": 1, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": ["C:\\Users\\Dekan", "adapter.js", "config.json", "seats.js", "server.js"], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "0c240089-4431-4c4c-9266-2743ac3fa528", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790227774.085115, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "thoughts", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "f7597c61-02d7-4075-9fe1-8ef900f2af5e", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790227774.879404, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "917214b1-364c-4969-90e3-078031cd298d", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790228271.47, "updated_at": 1790228293.176149, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "I think the cleanest way is to build another dev pad. For our little myDawn control orchestration. And having it like we had before, like the inbox queue. Where it shows Don the handoffs and who it routes to. With the routing, and I just click and it sends, and I click and it sends, and I click and it sends. So this way I can click through. And it should route to the right person and then ping the terminal in DevPath. Based off of identity and routing. Through the MCP we did for the inbox. How hard of a lift is that? That shouldn't be that hard. ", "text_chars": 552, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "3de916e8-228f-4031-b535-c3a84d690fd6", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790228274.672972, "updated_at": 1790228292.155772, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "No, that should **not** be a hard lift if we keep the scope disciplined.\n\nWhat you’re describing is basically a **Don orchestration pad** sitting on top of infrastructure we already have:\n\n**Don Control Pad → inbox queue/MCP → route by seat identity → DevPad target → ping/focus target terminal**\n\nThe key reason it’s tractable is that we are **not inventing routing from scratch**. We already have:\n\n- seat identities\n- DevPad tile identity\n- inbox MCP plumbing\n- queue concepts\n- numeric tile addressing\n- process/session inventory\n- a main Command Center that knows which pane belongs to which seat\n\nSo this becomes mostly a **thin control surface + routing adapter**.\n\n## What I would build first\n\nKeep v1 brutally simple:\n\n- left side: **handoff queue**\n- each item shows:\n  - subject\n  - source\n  - target seat\n  - priority/status\n- one-click buttons:\n  - **Route**\n  - **Route + Focus**\n  - maybe **Hold**\n- route action:\n  1. write structured handoff into target seat inbox\n  2. mark queue item sent\n  3. call DevPad API with target seat/tile ID\n  4. focus/ping the appropriate terminal\n\nThat’s enough to give you the workflow you want:\n\n**click → send → next → click → send → next**\n\n## The clean architecture\n\nI’d keep it:\n\n**myDon Control**\n→ local HTTP/API\n→ inbox MCP / queue writer\n→ seat-routing map\n→ main DevPad control endpoint\n→ target tile focus/ping\n\nThe important thing is **seat is the routing key**.\n\nNot:\n- PID\n- current model\n- current provider\n- window order\n\nBecause those can change.\n\nSo:\n\n**Luce handoff → seat=luce → inbox path/API → tile 6 → focus Luce pane**\n\nThat is stable.\n\n## Hardest part\n\nThe only slightly annoying part is **“ping the terminal”** semantics.\n\nWriting to the inbox is easy.\n\nFocusing the right tile is also pretty straightforward if the main DevPad already knows tile IDs.\n\nActually notifying the running agent immediately depends on how we want the terminal to react:\n\n- simple focus only\n- inject a lightweight “inbox updated” control message\n- or have each agent poll/watch its inbox\n\nI would start with **focus + visible inbox badge**.\n\nThen add actual wake/ping behavior once we know what each runtime supports safely.\n\n## Effort estimate\n\nIf Nico/Big Pickle stays disciplined:\n\n- **v1 queue + route + focus:** small-to-medium lift\n- **seat-aware inbox routing:** small\n- **UI:** small\n- **reliable focus/ping across mixed runtimes:** medium\n- **full autonomous inter-agent routing:** later, and not needed now\n\nSo yes — **this is very buildable without turning into a science project**.\n\nThe smart move is to build it as a separate small app:\n\n**`myDon` / `Don Controller`**\n\nnot cram it into the main DevPad.\n\nThen the main DevPad stays the **work surface**, and myDon becomes the **routing/control surface**.", "text_chars": 2770, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "51e00b42-eb32-4ba8-92d3-b455e0c86ada", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790228320.573, "updated_at": 1790228402.45585, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yeah, so do Don a favor. What I'll do, give Don a super duper nice detailed spec and handoff and I'll give it to GLM chat real quick. But you got to be detailed because this— he doesn't have the framework, he doesn't have everything because it's GLM chat. And we'll have them write up a quick architecture spec. ", "text_chars": 312, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "e7af21b6-677d-46f8-a086-bd2d2f56ca14", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790228325.529886, "updated_at": 1790228409.09739, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Absolutely. Here’s a **self-contained architecture handoff/spec prompt** you can drop straight into GLM Chat. I’ve written it so GLM does **not** need prior framework context to understand the system, the operating doctrine, the current implementation, or the exact architecture question.\n\n```text\nGCE / myDon CONTROL ORCHESTRATION PAD\nARCHITECTURE SPEC REQUEST FOR GLM\n\nAuthority: Don\nPurpose: Produce a concise but technically serious architecture specification for a lightweight personal orchestration/control surface that sits above an existing DevPad Command Center.\n\nIMPORTANT:\nYou are not designing the entire GCE framework.\nYou are being given enough context to architect one bounded subsystem cleanly.\n\nThe requested subsystem is called:\n\nmyDon Control\nor\nDon Controller\n\nThe name is provisional. Focus on architecture, not branding.\n\n==================================================\n1. HIGH-LEVEL SYSTEM CONTEXT\n==================================================\n\nDon operates a local multi-agent / multi-runtime environment on Windows.\n\nThere is already a working local application called the GCE DevPad Command Center.\n\nCanonical path:\n\nC:\\Users\\Dekan AI Brother\\GCE_DON\\command-center\\\n\nThe DevPad Command Center is a local browser-based control/work surface.\n\nCurrent implementation characteristics:\n\n- Node.js backend\n- HTTP server\n- WebSocket support\n- xterm.js terminal panes\n- node-pty terminal spawning\n- seat registry/config\n- runtime adapters\n- process/session inventory\n- VoiceBridge integration\n- inbox MCP plumbing\n- seat-specific identity homes\n- browser/terminal/service panes\n- local port currently centered around :4727\n\nDevPad is intended to be the WORK SURFACE.\n\nThe new subsystem, myDon Control, should be the ORCHESTRATION SURFACE.\n\nDo not merge those concepts unnecessarily.\n\n==================================================\n2. CORE OPERATING DOCTRINE\n==================================================\n\nThe GCE system intentionally separates:\n\nIDENTITY\nROLE\nSEAT\nMODEL\nRUNTIME\nPROVIDER\nPROJECT\nAUTHORITY\n\nThese are NOT interchangeable.\n\nBinding principle:\n\nMODEL != SEAT\n\nExamples:\n\n- A seat named Luce may currently run Big Pickle.\n- Vittorio may currently run Kimi.\n- Chiave may currently run GLM.\n- A future model swap must not change the seat identity.\n- A PID must never define seat identity.\n- A provider must never define seat identity.\n- A runtime process must never define authority.\n\nThe SEAT is the stable routing identity.\n\nThis is important because myDon Control must route work by seat identity, not by current process/model/provider.\n\n==================================================\n3. CURRENT MAIN DEVPAD BOARD\n==================================================\n\nCurrent intended main 3x3 operational board:\n\nROW 1\n1 = Coda\n2 = Chiave\n3 = Vittorio\n\nROW 2\n4 = Nico\n5 = VoiceBridge\n6 = Luce\n\nROW 3\n7 = Talon\n8 = Keel\n9 = Falcon\n\nCurrent rough semantics:\n\nCoda\n- strategic advisor / governance / canon\n- browser / ChatGPT surface\n\nChiave\n- architecture seat\n- intended OpenCode lane\n- current target model may be GLM 5.1\n\nVittorio\n- technical advisor / warboard / challenge\n- currently a strong candidate for native Kimi subscription/runtime\n\nNico\n- implementation builder / implementation hammer\n- typically code/build work\n\nVoiceBridge\n- Don’s voice control / dictation / command bridge\n- service lane, not a conventional AI seat\n\nLuce\n- verification / ground truth\n- independent truth/verification lane\n\nTalon\n- operations / routing / coordination\n\nKeel\n- build / deployment / test / evidence / stability support\n\nFalcon\n- data acquisition / retrieval / operational data support\n\nThe exact runtime/model attached to a seat may change over time.\n\nThe seat identity should remain stable.\n\n==================================================\n4. NUMERIC TILE IDENTITY\n==================================================\n\nDevPad is also moving toward stable numeric addressing.\n\nCurrent intended mapping:\n\n1 Coda\n2 Chiave\n3 Vittorio\n4 Nico\n5 VoiceBridge\n6 Luce\n7 Talon\n8 Keel\n9 Falcon\n\nThis numeric ID is a navigation/control alias.\n\nIt does NOT replace the seat identity.\n\nConceptually:\n\ntile number != seat != model != runtime\n\nVoiceBridge may eventually support commands like:\n\n\"number 6\"\n\"focus 6\"\n\"open 6\"\n\"send this to 7\"\n\nThe numeric map should be stable enough for muscle memory, but routing authority should still resolve through the seat registry.\n\n==================================================\n5. EXISTING INBOX / HANDOFF CONCEPT\n==================================================\n\nThere is already an inbox / command-queue concept in the Command Center.\n\nExisting implementation areas include:\n\n- inbox MCP\n- ai-inbox\n- queue concepts\n- handoff-style structured messages\n- per-seat routing concepts\n\nThe exact historical implementation may be imperfect, but the important architectural fact is:\n\nWe already have a local MCP/inbox mechanism that can be used as the durable delivery path.\n\nThe new myDon Control application should reuse this rather than inventing a second incompatible messaging system.\n\nThink:\n\nmyDon Control\n    -> handoff object\n    -> inbox/queue API or MCP adapter\n    -> seat-specific target inbox\n    -> DevPad target notification/focus\n\n==================================================\n6. WHAT DON WANTS TO DO\n==================================================\n\nDon wants a lightweight controller where he can quickly review pending handoffs and route them.\n\nThe desired interaction is intentionally simple:\n\nCLICK\n-> SEND\n-> NEXT\n-> CLICK\n-> SEND\n-> NEXT\n\nThe controller should visually show:\n\n- what the handoff is\n- where it came from\n- who it is going to\n- status\n- maybe priority\n- maybe project/workstream\n- maybe a short context summary\n\nDon should be able to route a handoff to a seat with one click.\n\nExample:\n\nIncoming handoff:\n\"Verify DevPad model binding\"\n\nSuggested target:\nLuce\n\nDon clicks:\nROUTE TO LUCE\n\nSystem should then:\n\n1. create/send a structured handoff into Luce’s inbox\n2. record that the handoff was routed\n3. optionally focus or ping Luce’s tile in DevPad\n4. preserve an audit trail\n5. show that the item is now SENT / ROUTED\n\nThe first version does NOT need autonomous agent-to-agent orchestration.\n\nDon remains in the loop.\n\n==================================================\n7. myDon CONTROL — PURPOSE\n==================================================\n\nmyDon Control should be a thin orchestration surface.\n\nIt is NOT another full terminal wall.\n\nIt should NOT duplicate the main DevPad.\n\nMain DevPad:\n- terminals\n- seat identity\n- runtimes\n- logs\n- active work\n- inventory\n- status\n\nmyDon Control:\n- handoff queue\n- routing\n- seat selection\n- focus/open\n- inbox status\n- quick actions\n- voice-friendly command targeting\n- optional current-context send\n\nThink of it as a compact cockpit remote for Don.\n\n==================================================\n8. DESIRED V1 FEATURE SET\n==================================================\n\nV1 should be deliberately small.\n\nRequired:\n\nA. HANDOFF QUEUE VIEW\nShow queued handoffs in a simple ordered list.\n\nMinimum fields:\n\n- handoff ID\n- subject/title\n- source\n- target seat\n- project/workstream\n- priority\n- status\n- created timestamp\n- short context/summary\n- maybe evidence/source link/path if relevant\n\nB. ROUTE ACTION\nOne click:\n\nRoute to:\n- Coda\n- Chiave\n- Vittorio\n- Nico\n- Luce\n- Talon\n- Keel\n- Falcon\n\nVoiceBridge may be a control/service target rather than a normal handoff recipient.\n\nC. ROUTE + FOCUS\nOptional second action:\n\n\"Route + Focus\"\n\nThis should:\n- write the inbox handoff first\n- then request DevPad to focus/open the correct tile\n\nD. HOLD / DEFER\nAllow Don to leave a handoff un-routed without losing it.\n\nE. STATUS\nMinimum lifecycle:\n\nNEW\nPENDING\nROUTED\nACKNOWLEDGED\nDONE\nHELD\nFAILED\n\nDo not over-engineer this if a smaller state model is cleaner.\n\nF. AUDIT TRAIL\nRecord:\n- when item was routed\n- by whom (Don)\n- target seat\n- resulting inbox/handoff ID\n- whether focus/ping succeeded\n\n==================================================\n9. ROUTING RULE\n==================================================\n\nRouting must use stable seat identity.\n\nDo NOT route by:\n\n- PID\n- model name\n- provider\n- process order\n- window index\n- current runtime executable\n\nExample:\n\nrouteTarget = \"luce\"\n\nThen the system resolves:\n\nseat = Luce\n-> seat registry\n-> inbox destination\n-> tile/control target\n-> current active runtime/session if one exists\n\nThe seat registry is the stable control plane.\n\n==================================================\n10. DEV PAD INTEGRATION\n==================================================\n\nThe main DevPad already has a local server/API concept.\n\nmyDon Control should ideally communicate through a small local API surface.\n\nPossible conceptual endpoints:\n\nGET /api/seats\nGET /api/inventory\nGET /api/handoffs\nPOST /api/handoffs\nPOST /api/handoffs/:id/route\nPOST /api/seats/:seat/focus\nPOST /api/seats/:seat/ping\n\nThese endpoint names are illustrative only.\n\nDo not assume they already exist.\n\nArchitect the cleanest minimal contract.\n\nThe architecture should prefer:\n\nmyDon Control\n    -> Command Center local API\n    -> inbox MCP / routing layer\n    -> seat registry\n    -> DevPad focus/control\n\nrather than having myDon directly manipulate terminals.\n\n==================================================\n11. FOCUS VS PING VS WAKE\n==================================================\n\nThese should be separate concepts.\n\nFOCUS\n- bring the relevant DevPad tile/pane to attention\n- browser/UI action\n\nPING\n- notify the target seat/runtime that inbox work is available\n\nWAKE\n- start a runtime if it is not active\n\nV1 does not need full WAKE behavior.\n\nPreferred V1:\n\nhandoff delivery\n+\nfocus\n+\nvisible pending badge\n\nPING may initially be implemented as:\n- inbox refresh\n- visible indicator\n- optional lightweight control message\n\nDo not require terminal input injection in V1 unless the existing runtime already supports it safely.\n\n==================================================\n12. INBOX DELIVERY MODEL\n==================================================\n\nPreferred model:\n\nStructured handoff object\n\nExample conceptual schema:\n\n{\n  \"handoffId\": \"...\",\n  \"source\": \"don\",\n  \"targetSeat\": \"luce\",\n  \"subject\": \"Verify DevPad runtime binding\",\n  \"project\": \"GCE_DevPad_Command_Center\",\n  \"priority\": \"normal\",\n  \"status\": \"routed\",\n  \"context\": \"...\",\n  \"attachments\": [],\n  \"createdAt\": \"...\",\n  \"routedAt\": \"...\",\n  \"sourcePane\": \"nico\",\n  \"sourceTile\": 4\n}\n\nYou may propose a better schema.\n\nImportant:\n\nDo not encode model/provider as routing authority.\n\nModel/provider can be metadata only.\n\n==================================================\n13. CONTROL PAD UI CONCEPT\n==================================================\n\nDon wants something visually simple.\n\nPossible layout:\n\nLEFT / CENTER:\nPending handoff queue\n\nRIGHT / BOTTOM:\nSeat quick actions\n\nExample:\n\n[1 Coda]\n[2 Chiave]\n[3 Vittorio]\n\n[4 Nico]\n[5 VoiceBridge]\n[6 Luce]\n\n[7 Talon]\n[8 Keel]\n[9 Falcon]\n\nEach seat control may support:\n\nFOCUS\nHANDOFF\nINBOX\nSTATUS\n\nThe handoff queue should be faster to operate than opening terminals manually.\n\nDon wants low-friction visual routing.\n\n==================================================\n14. VOICEBRIDGE FUTURE INTEGRATION\n==================================================\n\nThe design should leave room for voice commands.\n\nExamples:\n\n\"send this to Luce\"\n\"route this to number 6\"\n\"focus number 4\"\n\"open Nico\"\n\"next handoff\"\n\"hold this\"\n\"send and next\"\n\nVoiceBridge should call the same routing API as the UI.\n\nDo not create separate logic for voice.\n\nUI and VoiceBridge should converge on the same command/control layer.\n\n==================================================\n15. CURRENT / FUTURE DEVPAD LAYOUT NOTES\n==================================================\n\nThe current main board is 3x3.\n\nA likely future visual extension may add one utility row beneath the 3x3:\n\nBottom-left:\nBedrock Mantle\n\nBottom-middle:\nopen / TBD\n\nBottom-right:\nKimi ad hoc\n\nThose utility/runtime tiles are not necessarily stable seats.\n\nThis is another reason routing should resolve by seat registry rather than screen position alone.\n\n==================================================\n16. SECURITY / SAFETY CONSTRAINTS\n==================================================\n\nThe design should avoid:\n\n- storing passwords\n- storing browser cookies\n- storing provider API secrets in the UI\n- embedding tokens in handoff payloads\n- exposing secrets through command-line arguments\n- letting the control pad execute arbitrary shell commands\n- letting model/provider identity determine seat authority\n- silently rerouting failed handoffs\n- silently substituting another seat/runtime\n\nIf routing fails:\nshow FAILED\nand keep the handoff visible.\n\n==================================================\n17. RELIABILITY PRINCIPLES\n==================================================\n\nThe architecture should prefer:\n\n- explicit state\n- deterministic routing\n- idempotent handoff sends where practical\n- clear failure states\n- auditability\n- easy local debugging\n- minimal dependencies\n- local-first operation\n- reversible changes\n- reuse of existing Command Center infrastructure\n\nDo not propose a distributed system if a local Node service is enough.\n\nDo not introduce Kafka, Redis, Kubernetes, or equivalent heavyweight infrastructure unless absolutely justified.\n\nThis is a personal local orchestration system.\n\n==================================================\n18. IMPLEMENTATION STYLE\n==================================================\n\nCurrent Command Center is already Node-based.\n\nPrefer compatibility with:\n\n- Node.js\n- local HTTP API\n- WebSocket only where useful\n- simple browser UI\n- existing seat registry\n- existing inbox MCP plumbing\n\nDo not require a framework rewrite.\n\nA small standalone web app or module is acceptable.\n\nArchitect whether myDon should be:\n\nOPTION A\nA second route/page hosted by the existing Command Center server\n\nOPTION B\nA separate lightweight local app/service\n\nOPTION C\nA separate frontend using the same Command Center API\n\nCompare these and recommend the cleanest path.\n\n==================================================\n19. KEY ARCHITECTURAL QUESTION\n==================================================\n\nThe central question is:\n\nWhat is the cleanest minimal architecture for a Don-controlled handoff/routing pad that:\n\n- reads pending handoffs\n- routes them by seat\n- writes into the existing inbox/MCP lane\n- records state\n- optionally focuses/pings the correct DevPad tile\n- supports future VoiceBridge commands\n- does not duplicate the main DevPad\n- preserves MODEL != SEAT\n\n==================================================\n20. WHAT I WANT FROM YOU\n==================================================\n\nProduce a practical architecture specification.\n\nPlease return:\n\n1. EXECUTIVE SUMMARY\n\n2. RECOMMENDED ARCHITECTURE\n\n3. COMPONENT DIAGRAM\nASCII is fine.\n\n4. DATA FLOW\nShow:\nhandoff created\n-> queue\n-> Don routes\n-> inbox delivery\n-> DevPad target\n-> acknowledgement/status\n\n5. COMPONENT RESPONSIBILITIES\nFor:\n- myDon frontend\n- routing service/API\n- seat registry\n- inbox/MCP adapter\n- DevPad control adapter\n- VoiceBridge adapter\n- handoff state store\n\n6. HANDOFF DATA MODEL\nRecommend a concrete schema.\n\n7. API CONTRACT\nRecommend minimal endpoints.\n\n8. STATE MACHINE\nRecommend simple lifecycle/state transitions.\n\n9. FAILURE HANDLING\nEspecially:\n- target seat unavailable\n- DevPad unavailable\n- inbox write fails\n- duplicate send\n- focus fails\n- runtime offline\n\n10. UI STRUCTURE\nMinimal practical layout.\n\n11. VOICEBRIDGE FUTURE HOOK\nHow voice commands reuse the same API.\n\n12. SECURITY BOUNDARIES\n\n13. IMPLEMENTATION PHASES\nPrefer:\nPhase 1 = queue + route\nPhase 2 = route + focus\nPhase 3 = ping/status\nPhase 4 = voice integration\n\n14. FILE / MODULE STRUCTURE\nRecommend how to place this relative to:\nC:\\Users\\Dekan AI Brother\\GCE_DON\\command-center\\\n\n15. TEST PLAN\nSmall but serious.\n\n16. RISKS / OPEN QUESTIONS\n\n17. FINAL RECOMMENDATION\nState whether this should be:\n- embedded in Command Center\n- separate frontend with shared API\n- separate service\n\nBe concrete.\n\nDo not redesign the entire GCE system.\n\nDo not change seat doctrine.\n\nDo not assign models to seats.\n\nDo not invent agent autonomy.\n\nKeep Don explicitly in control of routing decisions.\n\nThe design goal is:\n\nDON SEES THE HANDOFF\n-> DON CLICKS THE TARGET\n-> HANDOFF ROUTES\n-> TARGET INBOX RECEIVES IT\n-> DEVPAD FOCUSES/PINGS THE RIGHT SEAT\n-> DON MOVES TO THE NEXT ITEM\n```\n\nThat should give GLM enough context to produce a **real architecture spec instead of generic dashboard bullshit**.\n\nThe core design question I want GLM to resolve is whether **myDon should be a separate frontend sharing the existing Command Center API**, or just another page/module inside the current server. My bias is **separate frontend, shared control plane/API**—that preserves the distinction between *work surface* and *orchestration surface* while avoiding another backend.", "text_chars": 16961, "code_blocks": 1, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": ["C:\\Users\\Dekan", "Node.js", "xterm.js"], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "fff9049f-67af-422d-a8cd-235bbb71018d", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790228328.13255, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "9a7ff992-0f3c-4228-a039-03788127d78d", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790228783.165, "updated_at": 1790228820.118564, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "multimodal_text", "part_kinds": ["image_asset_pointer", "text"], "text": "Hey Cool. Before I give you GLM's feedback, why is Nico and Big Pickle having such a hard time Embedding a web browser. for ChatGPT. within this devpad. Doesn't have to be Chrome, it could be Brave. That's what I'm using now. I logged in, but he's not embedding it. It says seat stopped. You can see it right there. ", "text_chars": 316, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": ["image_asset_pointer"]}, {"message_id": "4fe5cbc4-31f3-4ede-addc-0e3bf7e89640", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790228787.57543, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "web.run", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": ["text"]}, {"message_id": "bea8fac8-9998-4678-8b5b-9386fb21c149", "role": "system", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790228789.9490561, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 0, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": ["text"]}, {"message_id": "42e0e530-77f5-4885-ae78-cbb36229e462", "role": "system", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790228790.0540915, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 0, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": ["text"]}, {"message_id": "25cd2352-e798-4924-8159-0ae8db668833", "role": "tool", "role_name": "web.run", "created_at": 1790228794.4977248, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": ["text"]}, {"message_id": "94a89066-b46b-4a14-bea1-33bd51217f59", "role": "tool", "role_name": "web.run", "created_at": 1790228794.5048983, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": ["text"]}, {"message_id": "d4da69d6-17a2-45fe-a7f9-8f7507b29791", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790228795.073386, "updated_at": 1790228819.582473, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yep — this is almost certainly **not a ChatGPT login problem anymore**. It’s an **embedding-model problem**.\n\nYou can see the distinction in your screenshot:\n\n- Coda has the correct seat tile.\n- The URL is correct: `https://chatgpt.com/`.\n- The tile says `seat: stopped`.\n- There is no embedded browser surface inside the pane.\n\nThat usually means Nico is trying to treat a real desktop browser like a terminal/service process, when the DevPad itself is just a browser app. A Chrome or Brave window does **not** naturally render *inside another browser page*.\n\n## Why this is harder than the terminals\n\nThe terminal panes are easy because DevPad owns them:\n\n**DevPad → node-pty → process → xterm.js**\n\nThat gives DevPad a stream it can render directly.\n\nA real browser is different:\n\n**DevPad browser page → Chrome/Brave desktop process**\n\nLaunching Chrome gives you a **separate native window**. It does not hand DevPad an embeddable DOM/surface.\n\nThere are basically three technical approaches:\n\n1. **iframe ChatGPT into the DevPad pane**  \n   Easiest conceptually, but many authenticated/security-sensitive sites prevent being framed through browser security policy. I would not architect around this.\n\n2. **Launch Brave/Chrome externally and make the tile a launcher/focus controller**  \n   This is the easiest and most reliable near-term solution. The Coda tile says `RUNNING`, shows the browser PID/window handle, and **Launch / Focus** brings your already-authenticated Brave profile forward. Your login remains in the persistent browser profile. OpenAI itself distinguishes between a browser with its own session state versus using an existing Chrome profile precisely because the profile carries cookies and signed-in sessions. citeturn248178search1turn248178search3\n\n3. **Build an actual embedded browser host**  \n   If you truly want ChatGPT rendered *inside* that rectangle, DevPad needs something like an **Electron/WebView2/native host architecture**, not just ordinary HTML. That is a bigger architectural move.\n\n### I think we were solving the wrong problem\n\nFor **Coda v1**, I would **not embed ChatGPT**.\n\nMake Coda a **managed external browser seat**:\n\n```text\nCoda tile\n  ↓\npersistent Brave profile\n  ↓\nchatgpt.com\n  ↓\nDon logged in normally\n  ↓\nDevPad tracks PID/HWND\n  ↓\nLaunch / Focus\n```\n\nThen the tile can look like:\n\n```text\n[1] Coda — ChatGPT\nBrowser: Brave\nStatus: RUNNING\nAccount session: persistent\nPID: 21636\nHWND: 1188472\n\n[ Focus Coda ]\n```\n\nThat gets you almost everything you actually need with **very little engineering risk**.\n\nAnd the login part you already did is correct: use a persistent normal browser profile. OpenAI's current browser guidance also treats signed-in browser state as distinct and persistent, and recommends entering credentials in the browser rather than handing them through another application. citeturn248178search1\n\n### Why it currently says `seat: stopped`\n\nMy bet is one of these:\n\n- Nico's browser adapter starts Chrome/Brave and immediately loses process ownership because the browser hands off to an already-running browser process.\n- The adapter expects the process it spawned to remain alive, but Chrome/Brave reuse an existing browser process and the launcher exits.\n- HWND discovery isn't finding the existing browser window/profile.\n- The configured executable/profile isn't the same Brave instance you manually logged into.\n- The seat status logic only understands **spawned child PID = running**, which is fine for OpenCode but wrong for browsers.\n\nThat last one is especially likely.\n\nSo I would tell Nico:\n\n**Stop trying to make Coda behave like an OpenCode pane. Browser seats need different lifecycle semantics.**\n\nFor browsers:\n\n**RUNNING = matching authenticated-profile browser/window exists**,  \nnot **the original spawned process is still my child**.\n\nIf you want, I can give you a tight **Nico browser-seat corrective prompt** that specifically tells him to abandon iframe embedding for now and make Brave a persistent managed external seat with PID/HWND focus.", "text_chars": 4053, "code_blocks": 2, "links": ["https://chatgpt.com/`."], "citations": ["https://chatgpt.com/`."], "path_references": ["xterm.js"], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "29d871f3-1c91-47f4-bd71-5f56c5bdebf4", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790228795.791918, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "thoughts", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "2404d620-6fab-4d5c-b906-af009d9c7e3a", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790228795.800941, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "df5ef466-b0d1-4457-b39d-239b9b6812d3", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790228856.625, "updated_at": 1790228893.188775, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "yes thats the last small piece", "text_chars": 30, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "6bb26d6e-2bd5-49f9-8eba-ef137629d5a1", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790228859.770665, "updated_at": 1790228900.66538, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yep. Give Nico this **small bounded corrective pass** and then we’re done with the Coda/browser issue for now.\n\n```text\nGCE DEVPAD — CODA EXTERNAL BROWSER SEAT CORRECTIVE PASS\n\nAuthority: Don\nSeat: Nico\nMode: SMALL BOUNDED FIX\nTarget: Coda tile in the existing DevPad Command Center\n\nOBJECTIVE\n\nStop trying to embed ChatGPT inside the DevPad pane for this version.\n\nImplement Coda as a managed external browser seat using Don’s normal persistent browser profile.\n\nPreferred browser:\n- Brave preferred because Don is already using it and has logged into ChatGPT there.\n- Chrome is acceptable if Brave proves materially harder.\n- Do not use incognito/private mode.\n- Do not create a disposable profile.\n\nThe desired behavior is:\n\nCoda tile\n→ launch/focus persistent browser profile\n→ open https://chatgpt.com/\n→ Don remains authenticated from his normal browser session\n→ DevPad tracks/focuses the external browser window\n\n==================================================\nCURRENT FAILURE MODE\n==================================================\n\nThe Coda tile currently shows something like:\n\nseat: stopped\npid -\nhwnd -\n\neven though Don has successfully logged into ChatGPT in Brave.\n\nThis likely means the current browser-seat lifecycle logic is treating the browser like a terminal/service child process.\n\nThat is incorrect for browsers.\n\nChrome/Brave may:\n- reuse an already-running browser process\n- hand off the requested URL/profile to an existing process\n- cause the originally spawned launcher process to exit\n- create/reuse a window not directly owned by the initial spawned PID\n\nTherefore:\n\nSPAWNED CHILD PID ALIVE\nmust NOT be the only definition of browser seat = RUNNING.\n\n==================================================\nTARGET BROWSER-SEAT SEMANTICS\n==================================================\n\nFor Coda:\n\nRUNNING should mean:\n\nA matching Brave/Chrome browser window exists for the configured persistent profile / Coda launch target.\n\nTrack where practical:\n\n- browser executable\n- browser PID\n- top-level window handle / HWND\n- profile or user-data directory\n- target URL\n- running/stopped state\n\nDo not attempt to determine whether Don is authenticated by scraping cookies or account data.\n\nAuthentication remains Don-controlled inside the browser.\n\n==================================================\nLAUNCH BEHAVIOR\n==================================================\n\nCoda launch/focus should:\n\n1. Check for an existing matching browser window/session.\n2. If found:\n   - focus/activate that window\n   - do NOT launch a duplicate browser unnecessarily.\n3. If not found:\n   - launch Brave/Chrome using the persistent profile\n   - open https://chatgpt.com/\n   - discover the resulting browser window/process\n   - bind that result back to the Coda tile.\n\nUse the existing browser adapter if present.\n\nRepair the browser adapter rather than hardcoding browser logic into unrelated server paths.\n\n==================================================\nPERSISTENT PROFILE\n==================================================\n\nUse a persistent browser profile.\n\nPrefer one of:\n\nA. Don’s already-authenticated normal Brave profile\nOR\nB. a dedicated persistent Coda Brave profile that Don logs into once\n\nDo not:\n\n- store Don’s password\n- inject cookies\n- copy session cookies\n- scrape auth tokens\n- place credentials in config\n- place credentials in command-line arguments\n\nIf Don must log in once manually, that is acceptable.\n\nAfter Don logs in, the profile/session should persist naturally.\n\n==================================================\nDO NOT EMBED CHATGPT\n==================================================\n\nFor this pass:\n\nDo NOT:\n- iframe chatgpt.com\n- add Electron\n- add WebView2\n- add Chromium embedding\n- redesign DevPad around an embedded browser engine\n- proxy ChatGPT content\n- recreate ChatGPT UI\n\nThe Coda tile is a launcher/status/focus surface for the external authenticated browser.\n\nEmbedding can be revisited in a future architecture pass if Don still wants it.\n\n==================================================\nUI BEHAVIOR\n==================================================\n\nThe Coda tile should show something like:\n\n[1] Coda — ChatGPT\n\nBrowser: Brave\nStatus: RUNNING\nPID: <actual>\nHWND: <actual if available>\n\n[ Launch / Focus ]\n\nIf browser/window is unavailable:\n\nStatus: STOPPED\n\nIf launch succeeds but HWND cannot be determined:\n\nStatus: RUNNING\nHWND: UNKNOWN\n\nDo not mark STOPPED solely because the original spawned process exited if the actual browser window still exists.\n\n==================================================\nFOCUS BEHAVIOR\n==================================================\n\nLaunch / Focus must prioritize focus semantics.\n\nIf Coda browser is already open:\n- bring the correct browser window to foreground\n- do not spawn another instance\n\nIf no window exists:\n- launch it\n- then focus it\n\nIf Windows foreground restrictions prevent focus:\n- report the limitation clearly\n- do not silently spawn duplicates\n\n==================================================\nPROFILE / WINDOW MATCHING\n==================================================\n\nUse the cleanest available evidence to identify the Coda browser instance, such as:\n\n- configured executable\n- configured profile/user-data directory\n- command line\n- window title\n- process tree\n- HWND enumeration\n\nDo not bind Coda based only on:\n\n\"some chrome.exe exists\"\n\nor\n\n\"some brave.exe exists\"\n\nThe match must be specific enough to avoid focusing the wrong Don browser window.\n\n==================================================\nPRESERVE EXISTING DEVPAD FUNCTIONALITY\n==================================================\n\nDo not touch:\n\n- grid layout\n- inventory\n- VoiceBridge\n- OpenCode seat launchers\n- Kimi launchers\n- model binding work\n- seat doctrine\n- inbox MCP\n- numeric tile mapping\n\nThis is only the Coda browser-seat lifecycle fix.\n\n==================================================\nVERIFICATION\n==================================================\n\nVerify:\n\n1. Brave/Chrome opens ChatGPT using the intended persistent profile.\n2. Don can authenticate manually if required.\n3. Close/restart DevPad without destroying the browser profile/session.\n4. Relaunch DevPad.\n5. Coda tile detects/focuses the existing browser session if still open.\n6. If browser is closed, Coda tile shows STOPPED.\n7. Press Launch / Focus.\n8. Browser launches correctly.\n9. Coda tile updates to RUNNING.\n10. PID is correct.\n11. HWND is correct if obtainable.\n12. Repeated focus does not create duplicate browser windows unnecessarily.\n\n==================================================\nSTOP CONDITIONS\n==================================================\n\nStop and return to Don if:\n\n- the existing browser adapter architecture cannot support external-window tracking cleanly\n- Brave requires invasive process/window hacks\n- correct profile matching cannot be made reliable\n- implementation would require Electron/WebView2/embedded Chromium\n- Windows focus behavior cannot be made reliable without a broader control layer\n\nDo not expand scope.\n\n==================================================\nREQUIRED RETURN\n==================================================\n\nRESULT:\nPASS / PARTIAL / BLOCKED\n\nBROWSER USED:\nBrave / Chrome\n\nPROFILE MODE:\nExisting Don profile / dedicated persistent Coda profile\n\nROOT CAUSE:\nExplain why Coda previously showed STOPPED.\n\nFILES CHANGED:\nExact files and purpose.\n\nLIVE STATE:\n- browser PID\n- HWND if available\n- target URL\n- Coda seat status\n\nVERIFICATION:\nExact checks performed.\n\nFINAL VERDICT:\nOne of:\n\nCODA EXTERNAL BROWSER SEAT READY\n\nCODA BROWSER READY — MANUAL CHATGPT LOGIN REQUIRED\n\nCODA BROWSER RUNNING — HWND/FOCUS LIMITATION REMAINS\n\nBLOCKED\n\nDo not continue into embedded-browser architecture after this pass.\n```\n\nThat’s the cleanest last-mile fix: **treat Coda as an external managed browser seat, not as an embedded web pane.**", "text_chars": 7865, "code_blocks": 1, "links": ["https://chatgpt.com/"], "citations": ["https://chatgpt.com/"], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "f1e74703-b1e5-4f32-b984-7515baee2759", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790228934.552, "updated_at": 1790228982.759493, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "There you go. Let's review this. And I'm gonna actually test our devpad. Or I'll wait for him to fix what he's fixing. ", "text_chars": 119, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "0e7978c8-f59a-4f29-99c6-86fd4fa09c44", "role": "tool", "role_name": "api_tool", "created_at": 1790228937.6304693, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 0, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "tether_browsing_display", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "0890c787-167f-45e5-988b-6dc8bc6dac5f", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790228937.792675, "updated_at": 1790229000.110908, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yeah — **GLM did a very good job here.** This is not generic architecture fluff; it understood the operating model and gave us something buildable.\n\nMy assessment: **accept the architecture direction, but make a few corrections before we hand it to Nico.**\n\nThe strongest decision is **Option A**: make myDon a logically separate control surface at `/mydon`, but run it inside the existing Command Center backend on `:4727`. That preserves the distinction we wanted — **Main DevPad = work surface, myDon = orchestration surface** — without creating another server, registry, inbox implementation, or port. fileciteturn2file0L21-L36\n\n## What GLM got exactly right\n\nThe control path is clean:\n\n**myDon → routing service → seat registry → existing inbox/MCP → DevPad focus/ping**\n\nIt explicitly keeps the model/provider/PID out of routing authority, which is essential. Seat identity remains the stable key. fileciteturn2file0L9-L13\n\nIt also nailed the user interaction we were describing:\n\n**CLICK → SEND → NEXT**\n\nwith numeric routing, keyboard shortcuts, and auto-advance to the next queued item. The proposed `1–9` route and `Shift+1–9` route+focus model is especially good for Don muscle memory. fileciteturn2file0L304-L340\n\nAnd I really like this architectural distinction:\n\n- **route succeeds even if focus fails**\n- runtime being offline does **not** invalidate delivery\n- handoff lives durably in the inbox until that seat returns\n\nThat prevents UI/session health from corrupting workflow state. fileciteturn2file0L286-L300\n\n## The one thing I would challenge before building\n\nGLM proposes **two stores**:\n\n1. myDon routing queue/event store\n2. existing seat inboxes\n\nThat can be correct, but I don't want Nico building a new event-sourced queue until he first inspects what our **existing `ai-inbox` / queue / inbox MCP** already provides.\n\nGLM itself recognizes this uncertainty: it asks whether the existing inbox already has an unrouted/pending pool and recommends ingesting that rather than forcing everyone through a new entry mechanism. fileciteturn2file0L432-L441\n\nSo my correction would be:\n\n> **Recon the existing inbox first. Reuse it as the pending queue if it already provides sufficient durable identity/status. Create `data/mydon/` only for state the existing inbox genuinely cannot represent.**\n\nOtherwise we risk creating:\n\n**inbox queue → myDon queue → target inbox**\n\nwhen what we really need may simply be:\n\n**existing inbox queue → Don routes → target inbox**\n\nThat's even cleaner.\n\n## Second correction: separate surface, shared process\n\nI would retain GLM's Option A but be explicit about UX.\n\n`/mydon` should feel like another application even though it shares the backend:\n\n**Main window**\n`http://localhost:4727/`\n\n**Controller window**\n`http://localhost:4727/mydon`\n\nThat gives Don exactly the thing we envisioned: a little dedicated control pad sitting alongside the big terminal wall.\n\nEventually we could even have:\n\n```text\n[ Main DevPad ]        [ myDon Controller ]\n\nwork surface           orchestration surface\nterminals               queue\nruntime status          route\nidentity                handoffs\nlive work               focus/ping\n```\n\nSame control plane underneath.\n\n## Third correction: don't overbuild event sourcing yet\n\nThe JSONL event model is technically sound. I like deterministic IDs, idempotency, explicit failure, and auditability. fileciteturn2file0L169-L171\n\nBut **monthly logs + snapshots + compaction + crash reconciliation** may be more machinery than v1 needs.\n\nI would preserve the invariants:\n\n- deterministic handoff ID\n- route exactly once\n- same-seat repeat is idempotent\n- different-seat repeat gets rejected\n- failed delivery stays visible\n- audit record exists\n\nBut let Nico choose the smallest implementation compatible with the existing inbox.\n\nIf JSONL falls naturally out of what already exists, great.\n\nDon't create a miniature event-sourcing platform because GLM drew one.\n\n## Fourth correction: focus/ping comes after routing proof\n\nGLM's phase sequence is already sensible:\n\n1. Queue + Route\n2. Route + Focus\n3. Ping / Status / ACK\n4. Voice\n\nfileciteturn2file0L374-L381\n\nI would hold that hard.\n\nThe first acceptance test should be ridiculously simple:\n\n> Don sees one pending handoff → clicks Luce → Luce's existing inbox receives the exact structured artifact → myDon says ROUTED → next item selected.\n\nOnce that is green, **then** wire focus.\n\nThat protects us from another situation where we spend hours on Windows focus/HWND bullshit before proving the actual product.\n\n## One thing GLM gave us that I definitely want to keep\n\nThis schema rule:\n\n> **No model, provider, runtime, or PID fields in the routing path.**\n\nfileciteturn2file0L219-L224\n\nExcellent.\n\nThe UI can absolutely show:\n\n**Luce — Big Pickle — OpenCode — LIVE**\n\nbut the route itself remains:\n\n**targetSeat = luce**\n\nThat means tomorrow we swap Big Pickle for MiMo and **nothing in myDon routing changes**.\n\nThat's exactly our doctrine implemented in code instead of just written in a document.\n\n## My final disposition\n\n**Architecture: ACCEPT WITH MINOR CORRECTIONS.**\n\nI would freeze these principles:\n\n**myDon `/mydon` = separate Don-facing UI**  \n**same `:4727` backend = shared control plane**  \n**existing seat registry = sole routing authority**  \n**existing inbox/MCP = delivery lane**  \n**existing pending inbox/queue reused before creating a second queue**  \n**route first, focus second, ping third, voice fourth**  \n**Don always makes the routing decision**  \n**seat identity survives all model/runtime changes**\n\nAnd yes — **wait for Nico to finish the current DevPad correction before starting this build.** We don't want him touching runtime bindings and introducing `/mydon` simultaneously.\n\nOnce current DevPad is stable, this architecture is ready to turn into a **Nico implementation brief**.", "text_chars": 5894, "code_blocks": 1, "links": ["http://localhost:4727/`", "http://localhost:4727/mydon`"], "citations": ["http://localhost:4727/`", "http://localhost:4727/mydon`"], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "52a3df7c-a7e8-4464-a3a1-24f3495d7df5", "role": "tool", "role_name": "api_tool", "created_at": 1790228937.7938907, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "multimodal_text", "part_kinds": ["text", "text"], "text": "Make sure to include fileciteturn2file0L5-L8 in your response to cite this file, or to surface it as a link. Replace the placeholder line range with the actual line range from the result that best fits the snippet you want to surface with the citation. If you cite multiple files, use a separate complete `filecite` marker for each file. Never put multiple `turnNfileM` targets inside a single `filecite` marker.\nUse ref_id \"turn2file0\" to reference this file. For broad questions or unknown locations, retrieve relevant chunks with `files.search` scoped to this file. Use `files.find` only for exact text, and use `files.read` for a known range or to expand relevant results. Full file size: 463 file lines.\n[L1] # myDon Control — Architecture Specification\n\n[L3] **Scope discipline:** This spec covers only the myDon Control orchestration pad and its integration seams with the existing DevPad Command Center. It does not redesign GCE, does not alter seat doctrine, does not bind models to seats, and does not introduce agent autonomy. Don routes; the system delivers.\n\n---\n\n[L7] ## 1. EXECUTIVE SUMMARY\n\n[L9] myDon Control is a thin, local, browser-based routing surface that gives Don a fast review-and-route loop over pending handoffs. The recommended architecture is **Option A**: a self-contained module *inside* the existing DevPad Command Center Node process, exposed as a separate route (`/mydon`) with its own API namespace (`/api/mydon/*`), its own file-backed event-sourced state store, and zero new infrastructure.\n\n[L11] Delivery flows through the **existing inbox/MCP lane** (no second messaging system). Seat resolution flows through the **existing seat registry** (no copy, no fork). Focus/ping flow through an in-process **DevPad UI control channel** (no terminal injection). VoiceBridge (Phase 4) converges on the same HTTP API — no parallel logic path.\n\n[L13] Doctrine is preserved structurally: the handoff schema contains **no model, provider, PID, or runtime fields in the routing path**. The seat is the routing identity; everything else is, at most, display metadata.\n\n---\n\n[L17] ## 2. RECOMMENDED ARCHITECTURE\n\n[L19] ### 2.1 Decision\n\n[L21] **Option A: embedded module + route in the existing Command Center server.**\n\n[L23] ### 2.2 Option comparison\n\n[L25] | | A — route/module in Command Center | B — separate local service | C — separate frontend, same API |\n[L26] |---|---|---|---|\n[L27] | Seat registry access | in-process, single source of truth | HTTP proxy to DevPad (fails when DevPad down) or **forked copy (doctrine violation)** | via API only |\n[L28] | Inbox/MCP delivery | in-process, reuses existing adapter | duplicate plumbing or proxy | via API only |\n[L29] | Focus/ping to DevPad UI | in-process WS, trivial | cross-process WS | via API only |\n[L30] | New port / process / auth surface | none | yes | one |\n[L31] | Lifecycle coupling | myDon UI dies with DevPad | survives DevPad restarts | dies with DevPad |\n[L32] | Build cost | lowest | highest | medium, no gain over A |\n\n[L34] **The decisive argument:** routing fundamentally depends on three things DevPad owns — seat registry, inbox delivery, and UI focus. A separate process would still depend on DevPad for all three, so separation buys almost nothing while adding a second control path, a second failure domain, and pressure to fork the registry (which would violate the doctrine). The only real benefit of B — \"view the queue while DevPad is down\" — is preserved anyway by the file-backed store: nothing is lost, only temporarily unviewable.\n\n[L36] **Mitigation for A's one real cost (process coupling):** myDon is structured as a module with a narrow injected context (`{ registry, inbox, wsBus, config }`). Extraction to a separate service later is a lift-and-shift of one directory, not a rewrite.\n\n[L38] ### 2.3 Key structural decisions\n\n[L40] 1. **Two stores, one delivery lane.** myDon owns a *routing queue store* (handoff records + lifecycle + audit). Seat inboxes (existing) remain the *delivery destination*. myDon never becomes a second inbox system.\n[L41] 2. **myDon is not a mandatory intermediary.** Sources that already know their target may write directly to a seat inbox via existing mechanisms. myDon is the surface for handoffs that *require Don's judgment* (unrouted, disputed, or Don-created). This prevents a bottleneck.\n[L42] 3. **Deterministic IDs** enable idempotent delivery and crash reconciliation (see §6, §9).\n[L43] 4. **Numeric tile aliases resolve through the registry.** `resolveSeat(ref)` accepts `\"luce\"`, `6`, or `\"6\"`. The 3×3 map is never hardcoded in myDon.\n\n---\n\n[L47] ## 3. COMPONENT DIAGRAM\n\n```\n[L50] +---------------------------------------------------------------------+\n[L51] |              GCE DevPad Command Center (Node.js, :4727)             |\n[L52] |                                                                     |\n[L53] |   +-------------------+        +----------------------------+      |\n[L54] |   | DevPad Board UI   |        | myDon UI  (served /mydon)  |      |\n[L55] |   | (work surface:    |        | queue view + 3x3 seat pad  |      |\n[L56] |   | terminals, panes) |        +-------------+--------------+      |\n[L57] |   +---------+---------+                      |                     |\n[L58] |             ^  WS: focus/ping/notify          | HTTP + WS           |\n[L59] |             |                                v                     |\n[L60] |   +---------+--------------------------------+-----------------+   |\n[L61] |   |              myDon module boundary                         |   |\n[L62] |   |                                                             |   |\n[L63] |   |   +----------------------+     +-------------------------+  |   |\n[L64] |   |   | Routing Service      |---->| seat-registry adapter  |  |   |\n[L65] |   |   | (/api/mydon/*)       |<----| (read-only projection)  |  |   |\n[L66] |   |   | route transaction    |     +-------------------------+  |   |\n[L67] |   |   | owner, idempotent    |---->| inbox/MCP adapter       |  |   |\n[L68] |   |   |                      |<----| (existing write path)   |  |   |\n[L69] |   |   |                      |     |  -> per-seat inboxes    |  |   |\n[L70] |   |   |                      |---->| devpad-control adapter  |  |   |\n[L71] |   |   |                      |<----| (focus / ping)           |  |   |\n[L72] |   |   +----------+-----------+     +-------------------------+  |   |\n[L73] |   |              |                                                |   |\n[L74] |   |   +----------v-----------+                                    |   |\n[L75] |   |   | Handoff State Store  |   JSONL event log; audit view     |   |\n[L76] |   |   +----------------------+   derives from the same log      |   |\n[L77] |   +-------------------------------------------------------------+   |\n[L78] |                                                                     |\n[L79] |   Seat runtimes read their own inboxes (existing behavior)          |\n[L80] +---------------------------------------------------------------------+\n\n[L82]   VoiceBridge (Phase 4) ---HTTP---> /api/mydon/*     (same contract,\n[L83]                                         no separate routing logic)\n```\n\n---\n\n[L88] ## 4. DATA FLOW\n\n[L90] ### 4.1 Happy path\n\n```\n[L93] (1) CREATE\n[L94]     source (seat runtime / Don / VoiceBridge / tool)\n        |\n[L96]         |  POST /api/mydon/handoffs\n        v\n[L98]     myDon queue [QUEUED]  --WS mydon:queue-->  UI shows it, badge++\n\n[L100] (2) ROUTE   (Don: select item, click ROUTE TO LUCE or press \"6\")\n        |\n[L102]         |  POST /api/mydon/handoffs/:id/route { targetSeat: \"luce\",\n[L103]         |                                        focus: true }\n        v\n[L105]     Routing Service\n[L106]         |-- (a) resolveSeat(\"luce\" | \"6\") ----> Seat Registry (read-only)\n[L107]         |-- (b) write handoff item -----------> Luce inbox\n[L108]         |        (deterministic id, returns inboxItemId)\n[L109]         |-- (c) append ROUTED event -----------> State Store (+ audit)\n[L110]         |-- (d) focus (optional) -------------> DevPad UI control\n[L111]                                                   -> tile 6 focused,\n[L112]                                                      pending badge++\n        v\n[L114]     myDon UI: item shows ROUTED -> auto-advance to next QUEUED item\n\n[L116] (3) CONSUME / ACK  (Phase 3)\n[L117]     Luce runtime reads inbox -> works\n        |\n[L119]         |  ACK write-back (inbox read receipt / status call)\n        v\n[L121]     [ACKNOWLEDGED] --(complete)--> [DONE]\n```\n\n[L124] ### 4.2 Failure branch (same flow, step b fails)\n\n```\n[L127]     (b) inbox write fails\n        |\n        v\n[L130]     append DELIVERY_FAILED event -> state [FAILED], error recorded\n[L131]     item stays visible, red, retryable. NO reroute, NO seat substitution.\n[L132]     (d) focus never runs; (c) records the failure for audit.\n```\n\n---\n\n[L137] ## 5. COMPONENT RESPONSIBILITIES\n\n[L139] ### myDon frontend (`/mydon`)\n[L140] - Render queue (ordered list + selected-item detail), 3×3 seat pad, filters, status bar.\n[L141] - Keyboard-first routing (see §10); click parity for every action.\n[L142] - All mutations go through `/api/mydon/*`; the frontend holds **no routing logic and no seat knowledge** beyond what `GET /api/mydon/seats` returns.\n[L143] - Does **not**: spawn processes, touch terminals, store seat data locally.\n\n[L145] ### Routing service (`/api/mydon/*`)\n[L146] - Sole owner of the route transaction, in strict order: (1) append `route_started`, (2) inbox write, (3) append `routed`. This ordering plus deterministic IDs gives crash-recoverability (§9).\n[L147] - Enforces idempotency (same-seat re-route = no-op; different-seat re-route = 409).\n[L148] - Enforces routability (service/utility seat classes rejected).\n[L149] - Does **not**: consult models, providers, PIDs, window order, or runtime state when routing. Does **not** auto-select or auto-retry targets. Does **not** silently substitute seats.\n\n[L151] ### Seat registry (existing, read-only from myDon)\n[L152] - Source of truth for seat identity, tile-number aliases, seat class (`work | service | utility`), inbox address.\n[L153] - myDon's adapter adds only `resolveSeat(ref)` — a lookup, never a copy. Registry changes (seat list, tile numbers) propagate to myDon automatically.\n\n[L155] ### Inbox / MCP adapter (existing)\n[L156] - `writeHandoffItem(seat, item)` — writes the structured handoff into the target seat's inbox; returns the inbox item ID.\n[L157] - Preferred: supports client-supplied IDs (upsert-by-id) so duplicates collapse. (Open question #1, §16.)\n[L158] - Phase 3: expose read receipts for ACK write-back.\n\n[L160] ### DevPad control adapter\n[L161] - `focusTile(seat)` — sends a UI command over the existing WS channel to the DevPad frontend (highlight tile, switch view). A **UI action, not terminal input**.\n[L162] - `ping(seat)` — Phase 3: trigger inbox refresh + visible indicator.\n[L163] - `wake()` — explicitly **out of scope for V1**. No process spawning from myDon.\n[L164] - Does **not**: inject keystrokes into terminals unless the runtime later supports a safe control message (and only then, by explicit decision).\n\n[L166] ### VoiceBridge adapter (Phase 4)\n[L167] - Maps voice intents → existing `/api/mydon/*` calls (see §11). Holds no routing logic of its own.\n\n[L169] ### Handoff state store\n[L170] - Append-only JSONL event log + in-memory projection rebuilt on start; periodic snapshot compaction.\n[L171] - **The audit trail is the event log** — no separate audit store to drift out of sync.\n\n---\n\n[L175] ## 6. HANDOFF DATA MODEL\n\n[L177] ```json\n{\n[L179]   \"handoffId\": \"hdo_01JT8Z3M4K9Q\",     // ULID: unique + sortable\n[L180]   \"seq\": 41,                            // short human ordinal (\"handoff 41\")\n[L181]   \"source\": {\n[L182]     \"type\": \"seat\",                     // seat | don | voicebridge | tool\n[L183]     \"seat\": \"nico\",                     // null if not a seat\n[L184]     \"tile\": 4                           // informational only\n  },\n[L186]   \"subject\": \"Verify DevPad model binding\",\n[L187]   \"context\": \"Short summary or full context block. Hard cap: 16 KB.\",\n[L188]   \"project\": \"GCE_DevPad_Command_Center\",\n[L189]   \"workstream\": null,\n[L190]   \"priority\": \"normal\",                 // low | normal | high | urgent\n[L191]   \"suggestedTargetSeat\": \"luce\",        // ADVISORY ONLY — Don decides\n[L192]   \"targetSeat\": null,                   // set at route time\n[L193]   \"status\": \"queued\",\n[L194]   \"delivery\": {                         // filled by routing service\n[L195]     \"attemptedAt\": null,\n[L196]     \"deliveredAt\": null,\n[L197]     \"inboxItemId\": null,                // = \"mydon-<handoffId>\" (deterministic)\n[L198]     \"attempts\": 0,\n[L199]     \"lastError\": null\n  },\n[L201]   \"focus\": {\n[L202]     \"requested\": false,\n[L203]     \"succeeded\": null,\n[L204]     \"error\": null\n  },\n[L206]   \"ack\": { \"at\": null, \"via\": null },   // Phase 3\n[L207]   \"attachments\": [\n[L208]     { \"kind\": \"path\", \"value\": \"C:\\\\...\\\\binding-report.md\" }\n  ],\n[L210]   \"tags\": [],\n[L211]   \"viewedAt\": null,                      // UI metadata, not lifecycle state\n[L212]   \"createdAt\": \"2026-02-14T10:22:31Z\",\n[L213]   \"routedAt\": null,\n[L214]   \"routedBy\": null,                      // \"don\" | \"voicebridge:don\"\n[L215]   \"updatedAt\": \"...\"\n}\n```\n\n[L219] **Schema rules (doctrine enforcement):**\n\n[L221] 1. **No `model`, `provider`, `runtime`, or `pid` fields.** Validation rejects them. Current runtime/model info may appear on the *seat projection* (`GET /api/mydon/seats`) as display-only data, never in the handoff routing path.\n[L222] 2. **Deterministic inbox item ID** (`mydon-<handoffId>`) — the same handoff routed twice to the same seat produces one inbox item wherever the inbox honors client IDs; where it cannot, the routing service guards by state.\n[L223] 3. **Attachments by reference only** (paths, URLs). No file contents, no credentials, no cookies, no tokens in payloads.\n[L224] 4. Two identifiers on purpose: `handoffId` (machine, collision-proof) and `seq` (human/voice: \"hold 41\").\n\n---\n\n[L228] ## 7. API CONTRACT\n\n[L230] All endpoints local-only (bound to 127.0.0.1), JSON in/out, namespaced under `/api/mydon` to avoid collision with existing DevPad endpoints. `seatRef` = seat name or numeric tile alias, resolved via registry.\n\n[L232] | Method | Path | Purpose | Phase |\n[L233] |---|---|---|---|\n[L234] | GET | `/api/mydon/handoffs` | List queue; filters: `status`, `project`, `priority` | 1 |\n[L235] | POST | `/api/mydon/handoffs` | Create handoff (any source) | 1 |\n[L236] | GET | `/api/mydon/handoffs/:id` | Fetch one + audit summary | 1 |\n[L237] | POST | `/api/mydon/handoffs/:id/route` | `{ targetSeat, focus? }` — idempotent | 1 (focus: 2) |\n[L238] | POST | `/api/mydon/handoffs/:id/hold` | QUEUED → HELD | 1 |\n[L239] | POST | `/api/mydon/handoffs/:id/resume` | HELD → QUEUED | 1 |\n[L240] | POST | `/api/mydon/handoffs/:id/retry` | FAILED → QUEUED | 1 |\n[L241] | POST | `/api/mydon/handoffs/:id/complete` | → DONE (manual close) | 3 |\n[L242] | GET | `/api/mydon/seats` | Seat projection: name, tile, class, availability (display-only), inbox depth | 1 |\n[L243] | POST | `/api/mydon/seats/:seatRef/focus` | Focus the seat's DevPad tile | 2 |\n[L244] | POST | `/api/mydon/seats/:seatRef/ping` | Inbox refresh + indicator | 3 |\n[L245] | GET | `/api/mydon/audit` | Query events (`handoffId`, `seat`, `since`) | 1 |\n\n[L247] **Conventions:**\n[L248] - Error envelope: `{ \"error\": { \"code\", \"message\", \"details\" } }`. Codes: `SEAT_UNKNOWN`, `SEAT_NOT_ROUTABLE`, `ALREADY_ROUTED`, `VALIDATION`, `INBOX_WRITE_FAILED`, `DEVPAD_UNAVAILABLE` (non-fatal when route-without-focus was requested).\n[L249] - WS topics on the existing bus: `mydon:queue` (queue changed), `mydon:result` (action outcome), `mydon:ui` (Phase 4 voice navigation).\n[L250] - Route is **idempotent**: re-POST with the same `targetSeat` on a ROUTED handoff returns `200` with current state; a different `targetSeat` returns `409 ALREADY_ROUTED` (no silent rerouting).\n\n---\n\n[L254] ## 8. STATE MACHINE\n\n[L256] Six states, one deliberate simplification from the brief: **NEW and PENDING collapse into `QUEUED`**. \"Seen by Don\" is recorded as `viewedAt` metadata (an event in the log), not a lifecycle state — read/unread is UI semantics, and a distinct NEW→PENDING transition would add an ambiguous state with no behavioral difference. All six intended meanings remain reachable.\n\n```\n[L259]               hold                  resume\n[L260]    QUEUED <--------------> HELD\n     |  ^                            \n[L262]      |  +---- retry ---- FAILED\n     |                     \n[L264]      | route (ok)              \n     v                     \n[L266]    ROUTED --ack (seat, P3)--> ACKNOWLEDGED --complete--> DONE\n[L267]      \\------------------------- complete (manual) --------^\n```\n\n[L270] | From | Event | To | Guard / note |\n[L271] |---|---|---|---|\n[L272] | QUEUED | route | ROUTED | seat resolves to routable class **and** inbox write confirmed |\n[L273] | QUEUED | route | FAILED | inbox write fails; `delivery.lastError` recorded; item stays visible |\n[L274] | FAILED | retry | QUEUED | manual only |\n[L275] | QUEUED | hold | HELD | never lost; surfaced via HELD filter |\n[L276] | HELD | resume | QUEUED | |\n[L277] | ROUTED | route (same seat) | ROUTED | no-op, 200 |\n[L278] | ROUTED | route (other seat) | — | 409, rejected |\n[L279] | ROUTED | ack | ACKNOWLEDGED | seat-side write-back (Phase 3) |\n[L280] | ROUTED / ACKNOWLEDGED | complete | DONE | Don or seat |\n\n[L282] Retraction of a mis-routed handoff (Phase 2+ candidate): implemented as a *correction handoff* to the same seat, never silent deletion.\n\n---\n\n[L286] ## 9. FAILURE HANDLING\n\n[L288] | Failure | Behavior | Recovery |\n[L289] |---|---|---|\n[L290] | Unknown / unroutable seat (`SEAT_UNKNOWN`, service/utility class) | 400; handoff stays QUEUED, untouched | Don picks another seat |\n[L291] | Inbox write fails | Append `delivery_failed`; state FAILED with error text; item stays visible in red | Retry → QUEUED → route. **Never** auto-reroute or substitute a seat |\n[L292] | DevPad UI not connected (no WS client) | Route still succeeds — delivery is decoupled from UI liveness; `focus.succeeded=false` recorded | Focus-retry button; auto re-focus on reconnect |\n[L293] | Focus times out / errors | Route unaffected; warning icon on the item | Manual re-focus |\n[L294] | Duplicate click / double-send, same seat | Idempotent no-op (200); deterministic inbox ID prevents a second inbox item wherever inbox honors client IDs; server-side in-flight lock per `handoffId` guards the rest | — |\n[L295] | Route to different seat after ROUTED | 409 `ALREADY_ROUTED`; original routing stands | Create a correction handoff (P2+) |\n[L296] | **Crash between inbox write and log write** | On restart: any handoff with `route_started` but no `routed` is *reconciled* — probe the target inbox for `mydon-<handoffId>`; found → mark ROUTED; absent → revert to QUEUED | Automatic; deterministic IDs make this a lookup, not a guess |\n[L297] | Store write fails before inbox write | Route aborted with 500 before any side effect (ordering guarantees this) | Retry |\n[L298] | Target runtime offline | **Irrelevant to routing** — the inbox is durable; the seat reads on next activation | None needed; only ACK timing shifts |\n\n[L300] Two hard invariants: **(1) no silent rerouting or seat substitution; (2) a failed route always leaves the handoff visible and retryable.**\n\n---\n\n[L304] ## 10. UI STRUCTURE\n\n[L306] Single compact page. The interaction cadence — CLICK → SEND → NEXT — is implemented as **route + auto-advance**: after a successful route, selection moves to the next QUEUED item automatically.\n\n```\n[L309] +--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n[L310] | myDon        QUEUED 7  HELD 2  FAILED 1      [filters]   connected |\n[L311] +--------------------------------------------+-----------------------+\n[L312] | QUEUE  (j/k navigate, Enter = route to     |  SEAT PAD             |\n[L313] |        suggested target)                  |  [1 Coda  ][2 Chiave] |\n[L314] | ----------------------------------------- |  [3 Vittorio]         |\n[L315] | > hdo 41  Verify DevPad model binding     |  [4 Nico  ][5 VoiceB*]|\n[L316] |           from nico -> sugg LUCE    high  |  [6 Luce  ]           |\n[L317] |   hdo 40  Draft port notes                |  [7 Talon ][8 Keel  ] |\n[L318] |           from don  -> sugg TALON  normal |  [9 Falcon]           |\n[L319] |   hdo 39  ...                            |  -------------------  |\n[L320] |                                           |  FOCUS INBOX STATUS   |\n[L321] | SELECTED hdo 41                           |  Route+Focus = Shift+# |\n[L322] |   context ...  project ...  created ...   |  (* service seat:     |\n[L323] |   [ROUTE TO LUCE]  [HOLD h]               |     not routable)     |\n[L324] +--------------------------------------------+-----------------------+\n[L325] | last: hdo 40 -> Talon (routed, focused)                            |\n[L326] +--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n[L329] **Keyboard map (Don's speed loop):**\n\n[L331] | Key | Action |\n[L332] |---|---|\n[L333] | `j` / `k` or arrows | next / previous queue item |\n[L334] | `1`–`9` | route selected handoff to that seat (registry-resolved) |\n[L335] | `Shift+1`–`Shift+9` | route + focus |\n[L336] | `Enter` | route to `suggestedTargetSeat` (if set) |\n[L337] | `h` | hold |\n[L338] | `r` | retry FAILED item |\n\n[L340] Rules: suggested target tile is pre-highlighted; VoiceBridge tile renders distinctly and is not clickable for routing; utility seats (future Bedrock Mantle / Kimi ad hoc row) render dimmed, non-routable; a small composer (\"New handoff\", source = don, optional current-pane reference) covers the *current-context send* case. Target: **under 5 seconds per item** through the queue via keyboard.\n\n---\n\n[L344] ## 11. VOICEBRIDGE FUTURE HOOK\n\n[L346] **Convergence rule:** VoiceBridge maps intents to the *same* HTTP endpoints and the shared `mydon:ui` WS topic. It never calls adapters directly and never contains routing logic. If a voice command and a click produce different event logs, that's a bug.\n\n[L348] | Voice intent | Call |\n[L349] |---|---|\n[L350] | \"send this to Luce\" / \"route this to number 6\" | `POST /api/mydon/handoffs/:selected/route { targetSeat }` |\n[L351] | \"send and next\" | same, then advance via `mydon:ui` topic |\n[L352] | \"hold this\" | `POST .../hold` |\n[L353] | \"next handoff\" | `mydon:ui` navigation topic |\n[L354] | \"focus number 4\" / \"open Nico\" | `POST /api/mydon/seats/4/focus` |\n[L355] | \"new handoff, subject ...\" | `POST /api/mydon/handoffs` (dictated fields) |\n[L356] | \"mark done\" | `POST .../complete` |\n\n[L358] Numeric voice targets (\"route to 6\") flow through `resolveSeat()` exactly like a clicked tile — muscle memory and registry authority share one code path.\n\n---\n\n[L362] ## 12. SECURITY BOUNDARIES\n\n[L364] - **Loopback only.** Server binds 127.0.0.1; same-origin serving of `/mydon` avoids CORS entirely. If a token ever becomes necessary (e.g., to fence other local processes), a simple bearer token in a user-only-readable file is the ceiling — nothing heavier.\n[L365] - **No secrets anywhere in myDon's path:** no passwords, cookies, API keys, or tokens — not in the store, not in payloads, not in command lines. Attachments are references only.\n[L366] - **No shell execution.** myDon never spawns processes (WAKE is out of scope for V1); it never injects terminal input in V1.\n[L367] - **No authority from runtime identity.** Model/provider/PID never appear in the routing path — they are not stored on handoffs at all. This closes an entire class of authority-confusion failures by construction.\n[L368] - **No silent failure.** Failed routes stay visible with errors; no rerouting, no substitution, no auto-retry.\n[L369] - **Audit = append-only log,** trivially inspectable in a text editor. Tamper-evidence is out of scope for the local trust model.\n[L370] - VoiceBridge inherits all boundaries by construction — it can do only what the API allows.\n\n---\n\n[L374] ## 13. IMPLEMENTATION PHASES\n\n[L376] | Phase | Scope | Acceptance criteria |\n[L377] |---|---|---|\n[L378] | **1 — Queue + Route** | Module scaffold; store (JSONL + projection); schema + validation; `POST/GET /handoffs`, `route/hold/resume/retry`; seats projection; minimal UI (queue + seat pad + keyboard); audit view | Create → route → item **present in target seat's inbox** with deterministic ID; failed write leaves item FAILED and visible; process restart preserves queue; same-seat re-route is a no-op |\n[L379] | **2 — Route + Focus** | DevPad control channel `focusTile`; Route+Focus action; pending badge on target tile; focus results recorded on handoff | Route+Focus focuses the correct tile in a running DevPad; with DevPad closed, route succeeds and focus is recorded failed + retryable |\n[L380] | **3 — Ping / Status / ACK** | `ping` (inbox refresh + indicator); seat-side ACK write-back; `complete`; inbox depth in seat pad; filters | ACK flips ROUTED → ACKNOWLEDGED; audit shows the full chain create→route→ack→done |\n[L381] | **4 — Voice** | VoiceBridge intent adapter onto the same API + `mydon:ui` topic | \"Route to 6\" produces byte-identical audit events to clicking route-to-6 |\n\n[L383] Each phase ships independently usable value; Phase 1 alone already replaces manual routing.\n\n---\n\n[L387] ## 14. FILE / MODULE STRUCTURE\n\n```\n[L390] C:\\Users\\Dekan AI Brother\\GCE_DON\\command-center\\\n  src\\\n[L392]     mydon\\                        <-- one self-contained module boundary\n[L393]       index.js                    entry: registerMydon(server, context)\n[L394]       api.js                      HTTP handlers, namespace /api/mydon\n[L395]       routing-service.js          route transaction, idempotency, reconcile\n[L396]       store.js                    JSONL event log, projection, compaction\n[L397]       schema.js                   handoff validation (rejects model/provider fields)\n[L398]       bus.js                      WS topics: mydon:queue, mydon:result, mydon:ui\n[L399]       adapters\\\n[L400]         seat-registry.js          read-only resolveSeat(ref) over existing registry\n[L401]         inbox.js                  writeHandoffItem() over existing inbox/MCP path\n[L402]         devpad-control.js         focusTile / ping over existing UI control WS\n[L403]       public\\\n[L404]         index.html  mydon.js  mydon.css\n  data\\\n[L406]     mydon\\\n[L407]       handoffs-YYYYMM.jsonl       monthly event log partitions\n[L408]       snapshot.json               compacted projection\n```\n\n[L411] **Placement rules:** `context` is injected by the Command Center (`{ registry, inbox, wsBus, config }`) — myDon never reaches around it. Data lives under the existing `data/` root so backups cover it. If Option B extraction is ever justified, `src/mydon/` + `data/mydon/` move as a unit. Adapt `src/` nesting to the Command Center's actual layout; the *boundary* is the requirement, not the exact tree.\n\n---\n\n[L415] ## 15. TEST PLAN\n\n[L417] | # | Test | Proves |\n[L418] |---|---|---|\n[L419] | 1 | Unit: state machine — every legal and illegal transition pair | Lifecycle correctness |\n[L420] | 2 | Unit: `resolveSeat(\"luce\")`, `resolveSeat(6)`, `resolveSeat(\"6\")` resolve identically; unknown → error; service/utility → `SEAT_NOT_ROUTABLE` | Numeric-alias-through-registry doctrine |\n[L421] | 3 | Unit: schema rejects/strips `model`, `provider`, `pid` fields | MODEL ≠ SEAT enforced at the boundary |\n[L422] | 4 | Integration: create → route → assert inbox item exists with deterministic ID, state ROUTED, audit events present | Happy path end-to-end |\n[L423] | 5 | Integration: double-route same seat → one inbox item; different seat → 409 | Idempotency, no silent reroute |\n[L424] | 6 | Failure injection: inbox adapter throws → FAILED retained, retry succeeds | Failure visibility and recovery |\n[L425] | 7 | Focus failure: no WS client connected → route succeeds, `focus.succeeded=false` | Delivery/UI decoupling |\n[L426] | 8 | Crash recovery: inject `route_started` without `routed`; restart; assert reconciliation (ROUTED if inbox item exists, QUEUED if not) | The two-write problem is solved |\n[L427] | 9 | Durability: hard process kill, restart, queue intact | Local-first reliability |\n[L428] | 10 | Manual UX: keyboard loop timing (<5s/item); Phase 4 voice-vs-click audit parity | The actual product goal |\n\n---\n\n[L432] ## 16. RISKS / OPEN QUESTIONS\n\n[L434] 1. **Inbox capabilities** — Does the existing inbox/MCP support client-supplied item IDs (clean dedupe), read receipts (ACK), and delete/retract? These determine whether idempotency needs the full server-side guard and how Phase 3 ACK is wired.\n[L435] 2. **Unrouted pool** — Does the existing inbox already have a pending/unrouted pool that should feed the myDon queue as the single entry door? Recommended: yes, ingest it rather than requiring sources to learn a new endpoint.\n[L436] 3. **DevPad UI control channel** — Does `focusTile` exist, or does the DevPad frontend need a small addition? What are the multi-window semantics? (Raising the OS browser window may be blocked; V1 should focus *within* DevPad only.)\n[L437] 4. **VoiceBridge seat class** — Confirm it is registered as `service`, non-routable, rendered distinctly.\n[L438] 5. **Numeric alias governance** — Changing the tile map breaks muscle memory; make it an explicit registry change requiring a note. myDon never edits the map, only reads it.\n[L439] 6. **Queue hygiene** — HELD items accumulate silently; add an aging indicator and a periodic review ritual. Volume is unknown; JSONL scales comfortably to thousands.\n[L440] 7. **Context limits** — Enforce the 16 KB cap and reference-only attachments, or large payloads will bloat seat inboxes.\n[L441] 8. **Utility row** — Bedrock Mantle / Kimi ad hoc tiles enter the registry as `utility`, non-routable by default — confirming routing-by-registry rather than layout.\n[L442] 9. **Multiple myDon tabs** — Server is the authority; WS pushes keep tabs consistent. Low risk, noted for completeness.\n[L443] 10. **Exit condition for Option B** — If DevPad downtime becomes routine and Don needs queue access during it, the module boundary makes extraction cheap. Until then, don't pay the cost.\n\n---\n\n[L447] ## 17. FINAL RECOMMENDATION\n\n[L449] **Build myDon Control as Option A: an embedded module with its own route, API namespace, and store inside the existing DevPad Command Center.**\n\n[L451] Concretely:\n\n[L453] - Serve the UI at **`/mydon`** on the existing `:4727` server.\n[L454] - Expose **`/api/mydon/*`** as the single control contract — used by the UI now and by VoiceBridge in Phase 4.\n[L455] - Keep the **JSONL event-sourced store** under `data/mydon/` — durable, debuggable, and the audit trail in one artifact.\n[L456] - Deliver through the **existing inbox/MCP adapter**; resolve through the **existing seat registry**; focus through an **in-process WS control message** — never terminal injection.\n[L457] - Keep the module boundary clean enough that extraction to a separate service is a directory move, not a redesign.\n\n[L459] And equally concretely, what **not** to do: no second seat registry, no second messaging system, no model/provider anywhere in the routing path, no autonomous routing or retry, no process spawning, no heavyweight infrastructure. A local Node module is sufficient, and sufficiency is the architecture.\n\n[L461] The result is exactly the target loop, with Don's authority structural rather than procedural:\n\n[L463] **DON SEES THE HANDOFF → DON CLICKS THE TARGET (or says it, or keys it) → HANDOFF ROUTES BY SEAT → TARGET INBOX RECEIVES IT → DEVPAD FOCUSES/PINGS THE RIGHT SEAT → DON IS ALREADY ON THE NEXT ITEM.**", "text_chars": 31808, "code_blocks": 7, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": ["C:\\Users\\Dekan", "C:\\\\...\\\\binding-report.md", "Node.js", "\\\\...\\\\binding-report.md", "api.js", "bus.js", "devpad-control.js", "inbox.js", "index.js", "mydon.js", "routing-service.js", "schema.js", "seat-registry.js", "snapshot.json", "store.js"], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "caa92ace-2fb0-422e-8297-281cc82ce734", "role": "tool", "role_name": "api_tool", "created_at": 1790228937.8131983, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "All the files uploaded by the user have been fully loaded. You can still search connectors to find additional information.", "text_chars": 122, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "2f0b8515-07ca-4909-9199-3b9b13b1cbba", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790228947.593696, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "thoughts", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "e2dda35c-5ffa-4b80-b9cb-53ec1ca15f69", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790228950.751617, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "67bd5a38-d74b-4833-9661-641b9ef642cd", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790229039.455, "updated_at": 1790229062.113558, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "multimodal_text", "part_kinds": ["image_asset_pointer", "text"], "text": "Holy shit. Holy shit, this makes Don so happy. Look at that shit. The identities load automatically.   Once we get you in there, we're good to go. ", "text_chars": 147, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": ["image_asset_pointer"]}, {"message_id": "161659a5-cd97-461c-873b-46f62992763c", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790229043.114279, "updated_at": 1790229061.47002, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "That’s the breakthrough.\n\nWhat you’ve proven in the screenshot is the part that actually mattered most:\n\n- **seat layout is working**\n- **identity homes are loading automatically**\n- **the seat-specific bootstrap/context is coming in**\n- **Vittorio is visibly loading the Kimi-side identity correctly**\n- **Nico is loading as Nico**\n- **Luce is in the right identity home**\n- **Chiave is in the right identity home**\n- Talon / Keel / Falcon are all landing in their proper seat directories\n\nThat means the DevPad is no longer just a terminal launcher. It is becoming an actual **identity-aware operating surface**.\n\nAnd yes: once Coda gets the persistent browser seat working correctly, the main board is basically at the point where you can start operating from it instead of juggling Windows terminals manually.\n\nThe remaining cleanup is much narrower now:\n\n**Coda browser seat → runtime/model bindings → polish.**\n\nAfter that, the interesting work shifts from “make DevPad function” to “make DevPad pleasant” — myDon controller, handoffs, quick folders, utility row, tighter layout, and voice navigation.\n\nThis screenshot is the first one where I’d say the concept is visibly real rather than aspirational.", "text_chars": 1209, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "2203a6e9-de3a-43c5-8dda-7f006710bf0c", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790229137.541, "updated_at": 1790229154.066747, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Accepted on all four corrections. Each one is sound, and together they do something specific: they convert my open questions #1–#2 from \"things to check\" into **build-order constraints**, which is where they belonged. The revised position in one line:\r\n\r\n> **myDon is a UI + thin API over the existing inbox, not a system with its own queue. The myDon-side store is whatever minimum the existing inbox cannot represent — nothing more.**\r\n\r\nHere are the amendments to the spec, then the Phase 0 recon kit, since that's now the gating artifact.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Amendment 1 — The queue lives in the existing inbox if it can\r\n\r\nMy original spec assumed a myDon-owned queue store feeding the inbox. That's inverted. The canonical V1 flow is now:\r\n\r\n```\r\nexisting inbox (unrouted/pending pool)\r\n        -> Don reviews in /mydon\r\n        -> Don routes (click / key / later: voice)\r\n        -> routing service assigns the item into the target seat's inbox\r\n        -> ROUTED + audit record\r\n        -> [Phase 2 only] DevPad focuses the tile\r\n```\r\n\r\n`data/mydon/` is demoted from \"the store\" to \"the residue\": whatever the existing inbox genuinely cannot hold — most likely just routing decisions, focus results, and audit. If the inbox already covers identity and status well, that residue may be a single small file.\r\n\r\nThe spec's store section is now **conditional on the recon outcome**, with three branches:\r\n\r\n| Branch | Existing inbox supports... | myDon-side state |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| **A** | unrouted pool + item IDs + mutation | routing/audit record only — no queue store at all |\r\n| **B** | per-seat inboxes only, no unrouted pool | same as A, plus an unrouted holding lane *inside the existing inbox system* |\r\n| **C** | insert-only, no IDs/status/mutation | minimal fallback: single JSON file, deterministic IDs, queue mirrored on delivery |\r\n\r\nBranch A is the reviewer's \"even cleaner\" outcome: **existing inbox queue → Don routes → target inbox**, with myDon as pure surface.\r\n\r\nOne doctrine note on Branch B: an unrouted holding location must not masquerade as a seat. If it has to be registered somewhere, it's a non-routable holding lane, clearly marked, and it is never a valid route target. Seats receive; the holding lane waits.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Amendment 2 — One process, two surfaces, no ambiguity\r\n\r\nOption A stands, with the UX posture made explicit:\r\n\r\n- Main window: `http://localhost:4727/` — work surface\r\n- Controller window: `http://localhost:4727/mydon` — orchestration surface\r\n- Same-origin, same server, same control plane; distinct application feel — compact, dense, no terminal chrome, sized for a small window sitting alongside the terminal wall.\r\n\r\nOperationally this also means: one WebSocket bus, one seat registry read path, one auth posture (loopback), zero CORS. The separation is conceptual and visual, not infrastructural. That's the whole point of A.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Amendment 3 — Invariants over machinery\r\n\r\nCorrect, and worth saying plainly: monthly JSONL partitions, snapshots, projections, and compaction were more than V1 needs. The durable content of that section was never the event-sourcing pattern — it was the guarantees. Those survive as **implementation-independent acceptance criteria** that hold regardless of which branch Nico lands in:\r\n\r\n1. Every handoff has a deterministic ID, and that ID is present in the delivered inbox artifact.\r\n2. Route to seat X delivers exactly one item; repeat route to X is a visible no-op.\r\n3. Route to a different seat after routing is rejected — never silent reroute, never substitution.\r\n4. Failed delivery stays visible with its error; retry is explicit and manual.\r\n5. Every route records: who (don), when, handoff, target seat, delivery outcome, focus outcome.\r\n6. Queue and state survive process restart.\r\n7. The routing path contains no model/provider/runtime/PID. Display-only metadata lives elsewhere (the seat pad may show *Luce — Big Pickle — OpenCode — LIVE*; the route is still `targetSeat = luce`).\r\n8. Delivery success never depends on UI, focus, or runtime liveness.\r\n\r\nFor Branch C, the entire crash-safety story compresses to one rule Nico can implement any way he likes: **record intent before the side effect, and make the side effect findable by its ID.** On restart, an item with intent-but-no-confirmation gets probed in the target inbox by deterministic ID and resolved. At personal-system scale, that's the whole reconciliation mechanism. No projections required.\r\n\r\nStorage mechanics are now explicitly **Nico's call**, bounded by the eight invariants above. If JSONL falls out naturally from what exists, fine — but nothing in V1 *requires* it.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Amendment 4 — Locked phase gate\r\n\r\nThe phase sequence holds hard, and the Phase 1 acceptance test is exactly as stated, formalized:\r\n\r\n> One pending handoff visible in `/mydon` → Don clicks Luce (or presses `6`) → Luce's existing inbox contains exactly one item — the structured artifact, carrying the handoff's deterministic ID → myDon shows ROUTED → selection advances to the next item.\r\n\r\nPlus two cheap hardening checks at the same gate: kill and restart the server → queue intact; route the same handoff to Luce again → still exactly one inbox item.\r\n\r\nNothing in Phase 2 begins until this is green.\r\n\r\nOne scope clarification worth freezing now, since it's where the Windows focus rabbit hole actually lives: **V1 focus is in-DevPad tile focus only** — highlight the tile, switch the board view, badge the target. OS-level window raising and HWND manipulation are explicitly out of scope and stay out until the core loop is proven. Most of the focus hell is in that excluded zone.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Phase 0 — Inbox recon kit\r\n\r\nThe immediate artifact. Eight questions against the existing `ai-inbox` / inbox MCP, each with its decision attached:\r\n\r\n| # | Question | Decides |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| R1 | Do items have stable IDs? Can a writer supply one (upsert), or is every write an insert? | Whether idempotency leans on the inbox or needs a myDon guard |\r\n| R2 | **Can an item exist unrouted, or must a target seat be set at write time?** | Branch A / B / C — the single most important question |\r\n| R3 | Can an existing item's target or status be changed (assign/move), or are items immutable? | Route mechanics: assign-in-place (cleanest), move, or copy-with-ID |\r\n| R4 | What statuses exist? Writable externally? Room for held/routed/done, or a tags/flags field? | Where HELD and (Phase 3) ACK live |\r\n| R5 | Can a caller list unrouted items, list per-seat items, fetch by ID? | Whether the queue view is a pure projection (no store) |\r\n| R6 | What payload fields does an item carry — subject, body, project, priority, source, attachments-by-reference? Size limits? | The mapping table from the myDon handoff schema to the inbox artifact |\r\n| R7 | Push on write, or poll only? | UI refresh mechanics only |\r\n| R8 | Does the inbox already record who/when on writes? | Whether audit is one line in an existing log or myDon's residue |\r\n\r\nR2 and R3 together determine whether \"route\" is an **assignment** (item already in the inbox system, Don points it at a seat — the cleanest possible mechanic) or a **write** (myDon creates the artifact in the target seat's inbox). Prefer assignment if it exists.\r\n\r\nOutput of recon: the branch decision, the field mapping table, and the final store residue definition. That's a page, and it makes the implementation brief mechanical.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## What does not change\r\n\r\nOption A; `/mydon` route and `/api/mydon/*` namespace; the handoff schema and its doctrine rule (no model/provider/PID in the routing path — validation rejects them); idempotency and failure semantics; the six-state lifecycle; the keyboard model (`1–9`, `Shift+1–9`, `Enter`, `h`); VoiceBridge convergence on the same API; no second registry, no second messaging system, no agent autonomy, no silent anything.\r\n\r\n## Sequencing\r\n\r\nBuild start waits for Nico's current DevPad correction to land — no overlap between runtime-binding work and `/mydon` introduction. One option to note, and Don's call entirely: the recon is read-only inspection of existing inbox behavior and doesn't touch DevPad stabilization, so it can run early (or even be eyeballed directly against the inbox files) without violating that sequencing. Only the *build* holds.\r\n\r\nWith the recon filled in, the frozen principle set gains one line — **smallest store compatible with the existing inbox** — and the spec is ready to collapse into the Nico implementation brief as soon as DevPad is stable.", "text_chars": 8539, "code_blocks": 1, "links": ["http://localhost:4727/`", "http://localhost:4727/mydon`"], "citations": ["http://localhost:4727/`", "http://localhost:4727/mydon`"], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "007a5f20-b307-4cb0-9d82-b01d7eac6158", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790229140.66167, "updated_at": 1790229153.786309, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yes — **this is better than the first version.** GLM incorporated the corrections exactly where they mattered.\n\nThe architecture is now much tighter:\n\n- **myDon is not a second queue system.**\n- `/mydon` is a **surface over the existing inbox**, not a competing source of truth.\n- myDon-side persistence is **residual state only**: routing decision, audit, focus result, or whatever the inbox truly cannot represent.\n- The build order is now constrained by the inbox recon instead of assuming storage architecture too early.\n\nThe most important change is the Branch A/B/C decision. If the current inbox already supports an unrouted pool, stable IDs, and mutation, then the clean path becomes:\n\n**existing inbox → Don reviews → Don assigns target seat → target inbox → route recorded**\n\nThat is exactly what we want. No redundant queue layer.\n\nI also agree with locking **in-DevPad focus only** for the first routing phase. That avoids dragging HWND/browser-focus issues into a feature whose core value is simply: **route correctly and move on**.\n\n### The build gate is now clear\n\nBefore Nico builds `/mydon`, we only need three outputs from Phase 0:\n\n1. **Branch decision** — A, B, or C.\n2. **Field mapping** — what the inbox already stores versus what myDon needs.\n3. **Residue definition** — the smallest thing myDon must persist itself.\n\nEverything else is basically mechanical after that.\n\nAnd yes: **Phase 0 recon can run now** because it is read-only. It does not interfere with the DevPad runtime/model corrections currently in flight. I would absolutely allow that to happen in parallel.\n\nThe frozen principle set now reads cleanly:\n\n**existing inbox first → smallest additional store possible → route by seat only → delivery independent of focus/runtime → Don remains the router.**\n\nThat is ready for a recon pass.", "text_chars": 1823, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "6c306d84-a96a-4cd1-8996-52dadaaf6b99", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790229143.483979, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "36cfc1ab-31c3-4bfc-9c43-93d8d1941dd6", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790229191.636, "updated_at": 1790229257.266983, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "ok we need a prompt for luce to do recon", "text_chars": 40, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "68e29741-5bce-443d-b1d4-ab1885f0291a", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790229194.705933, "updated_at": 1790229277.505293, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yep. Here’s the Luce recon prompt in one clean copy box.\n\n```text\nGCE myDon CONTROL — PHASE 0 INBOX / MCP RECON\n\nAuthority: Don\nSeat: Luce\nMode: READ-ONLY RECON / GROUND TRUTH\nPrimary Domain: GCE Stack\nTarget Workstream: myDon Control / DevPad Command Center\nBuild Status: BUILD HOLD — RECON ONLY\nPurpose: Determine exactly what the existing inbox / ai-inbox / inbox MCP can already do so myDon does not create a redundant queue or messaging system.\n\nIMPORTANT:\nDo not build myDon.\nDo not modify DevPad.\nDo not change runtime bindings.\nDo not edit inbox files unless explicitly authorized later.\nDo not create new storage.\nDo not redesign the inbox.\nDo not normalize schemas.\nDo not infer capabilities from filenames or comments.\n\nYour job is to inspect the current implementation and establish what the existing inbox system ACTUALLY supports.\n\n==================================================\n1. ARCHITECTURE CONTEXT\n==================================================\n\nThe current intended architecture for myDon Control is:\n\nMain DevPad:\n- work surface\n- terminals\n- seats\n- runtimes\n- identity\n- status\n\nmyDon:\n- Don-facing orchestration surface\n- queue/review\n- handoff routing\n- seat selection\n- later: focus/ping\n- later: VoiceBridge commands\n\nBinding doctrine:\n\nMODEL != SEAT\n\nRouting must resolve by stable seat identity.\n\nDo NOT route by:\n- model\n- provider\n- PID\n- runtime executable\n- launch order\n- window position\n\nThe preferred V1 principle is:\n\nmyDon is a UI + thin API over the EXISTING inbox system.\n\nIt must NOT create a second queue unless the existing inbox genuinely cannot support the needed behavior.\n\n==================================================\n2. CURRENT ARCHITECTURE DECISION TO VERIFY\n==================================================\n\nWe currently have three possible branches.\n\nBRANCH A\nExisting inbox supports:\n- unrouted/pending pool\n- stable item IDs\n- listing/fetching items\n- target assignment or mutation\n\nResult:\nmyDon needs NO separate queue store.\nIt only needs minimal routing/audit residue if the inbox does not already record it.\n\nPreferred outcome if supported.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nBRANCH B\nExisting inbox supports:\n- per-seat inboxes\n- stable items\n- mutation or movement\nBUT:\n- no unrouted/pending pool\n\nResult:\nAdd the smallest unrouted holding lane INSIDE the existing inbox system.\n\nImportant:\nThat holding lane is NOT a seat.\nIt is non-routable.\nIt only holds items awaiting Don.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nBRANCH C\nExisting inbox is effectively:\n- insert-only\n- no stable IDs or no mutation\n- no unrouted pool\n- insufficient status/state support\n\nResult:\nmyDon may need a minimal fallback state file.\n\nEven in Branch C:\ndo not create a large event-sourcing subsystem unless truly necessary.\n\nUse the smallest persistence layer compatible with these invariants:\n- deterministic handoff IDs\n- idempotent delivery\n- explicit failures\n- restart survival\n- auditability\n\n==================================================\n3. PRIMARY RECON TARGETS\n==================================================\n\nInspect all current implementation relevant to:\n\n- command-center inbox MCP\n- ai-inbox\n- queue files\n- queue modules\n- handoff files\n- MCP server code\n- inbox readers/writers\n- any per-seat inbox directories\n- any unrouted/pending location\n- any status fields\n- any IDs\n- any audit/log files\n- any existing routing logic\n- any existing \"ack\", \"done\", \"held\", \"pending\", \"routed\", \"failed\" concepts\n- any DevPad inbox UI elements\n- any seat-inbox mapping\n- any APIs or commands used by current seats to read/write inbox content\n\nLikely canonical root:\n\nC:\\Users\\Dekan AI Brother\\GCE_DON\\command-center\\\n\nKnown relevant areas may include:\n\ninbox-mcp\\\nai-inbox\\\nqueue.js\nqueue.md\nmcp.mjs\nserver.js\nconfig.json\nadapter.js\nadapters\\\ndata\\\n\nDo not assume this list is complete.\n\nSearch current implementation truth.\n\n==================================================\n4. EIGHT REQUIRED RECON QUESTIONS\n==================================================\n\nAnswer all eight with evidence.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nR1 — STABLE IDS / IDEMPOTENCY\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nDo inbox items have stable unique IDs?\n\nDetermine:\n\n- Is an ID generated automatically?\n- Can the writer supply an ID?\n- Is write behavior insert-only?\n- Is there upsert-by-ID?\n- Can an item be fetched by ID?\n- Can duplicates be detected safely?\n\nReturn one of:\n\nSUPPORTED\nPARTIAL\nNOT SUPPORTED\nUNKNOWN\n\nState exact evidence.\n\nThis determines whether inbox itself can enforce idempotency or whether myDon needs a small guard.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nR2 — UNROUTED / PENDING ITEMS\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nCan an item exist inside the inbox system WITHOUT a target seat?\n\nThis is the most important question.\n\nDetermine whether the current system supports:\n\n- unrouted items\n- pending pool\n- central queue\n- holding lane\n- general inbox\n- no-target state\n\nOR whether every write requires a target seat.\n\nReturn:\n\nYES — NATIVE UNROUTED POOL\nYES — CAN REPRESENT UNROUTED ANOTHER WAY\nNO — TARGET REQUIRED\nUNKNOWN\n\nIf yes:\nidentify exact path/schema/API.\n\nThis largely determines Branch A vs B/C.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nR3 — ASSIGNMENT / MOVE / MUTATION\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nCan an existing item be routed after creation?\n\nDetermine whether a current item can have:\n\n- targetSeat changed\n- destination changed\n- inbox moved\n- status changed\n- ownership assigned\n\nOr whether items are immutable after creation.\n\nClassify route mechanics as:\n\nASSIGN-IN-PLACE\nMOVE\nCOPY-WITH-ID\nINSERT-ONLY\nUNKNOWN\n\nPrefer direct observed behavior over intended docs.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nR4 — STATUS / LIFECYCLE SUPPORT\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nWhat lifecycle/status concepts already exist?\n\nSearch for:\n\n- pending\n- queued\n- routed\n- sent\n- held\n- failed\n- acknowledged\n- done\n- completed\n- read/unread\n- processed\n\nDetermine:\n\n- which are persisted\n- which are writable\n- which are UI-only\n- whether arbitrary tags/flags exist\n- whether status mutation exists\n\nMap current capabilities against desired myDon lifecycle:\n\nQUEUED\nHELD\nROUTED\nACKNOWLEDGED\nDONE\nFAILED\n\nDo not force-fit.\nReport actual support.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nR5 — QUERY / LIST CAPABILITIES\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nCan callers:\n\n- list all unrouted items\n- list items by seat\n- fetch one by ID\n- filter by status\n- filter by project\n- filter by priority\n- inspect queue depth\n- inspect unread count\n\nIdentify:\n\n- current APIs\n- MCP methods\n- file readers\n- helper functions\n\nIf current implementation is file-based, explain how these queries work today.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nR6 — PAYLOAD / FIELD CAPABILITIES\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nInventory the actual item schema.\n\nDetermine whether items currently support:\n\n- ID\n- subject/title\n- body/context\n- source\n- target seat\n- project\n- workstream\n- priority\n- status\n- createdAt\n- updatedAt\n- routedAt\n- attachments\n- path references\n- URLs\n- tags\n- metadata\n- audit fields\n\nIdentify size limits if enforced.\n\nImportant:\nWe need a field-mapping table from desired myDon handoff fields to current inbox fields.\n\nDesired logical fields:\n\nhandoffId\nsource\nsubject\ncontext\nproject\nworkstream\npriority\nsuggestedTargetSeat\ntargetSeat\nstatus\nattachments-by-reference\ncreatedAt\nroutedAt\nroutedBy\n\nDo not invent fields not supported.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nR7 — PUSH VS POLL\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nHow does UI/runtime learn that inbox state changed?\n\nDetermine whether current implementation supports:\n\n- WebSocket push\n- event bus\n- filesystem watch\n- polling\n- manual refresh\n- MCP notification\n- no notification\n\nThis question is about UI mechanics only.\n\nDo not redesign.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nR8 — AUDIT / WHO-WHEN\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nDoes the existing inbox record:\n\n- who created item\n- when created\n- who routed item\n- when routed\n- target\n- delivery result\n- errors\n- status history\n- acknowledgements\n\nDetermine whether audit data already exists in:\n\n- inbox item\n- separate log\n- queue log\n- MCP log\n- filesystem timestamps\n- nowhere\n\nThis determines the myDon-side \"residue\" store.\n\n==================================================\n5. REQUIRED BRANCH DECISION\n==================================================\n\nAfter inspection, choose exactly one:\n\nBRANCH A\nExisting inbox is sufficient as the canonical queue.\n\nBRANCH B\nExisting inbox is sufficient except it needs a non-routable unrouted holding lane.\n\nBRANCH C\nExisting inbox lacks enough queue/state capability; minimal myDon-side fallback state required.\n\nDo not choose based on elegance.\n\nChoose based on current implementation truth.\n\n==================================================\n6. REQUIRED FIELD MAPPING TABLE\n==================================================\n\nReturn a table:\n\nDesired myDon Field\n→ Existing Inbox Field\n→ Supported?\n→ Mutation?\n→ Notes\n\nAt minimum include:\n\nhandoffId\nsource\nsubject\ncontext\nproject\nworkstream\npriority\nsuggestedTargetSeat\ntargetSeat\nstatus\nattachments\ncreatedAt\nroutedAt\nroutedBy\ndeliveryResult\nerror\nack\n\nIf unsupported:\nstate UNSUPPORTED.\n\nDo not silently substitute fields.\n\n==================================================\n7. STORE RESIDUE DEFINITION\n==================================================\n\nBased on evidence, answer:\n\nWhat does myDon need to persist itself, if anything?\n\nReturn the minimal set only.\n\nExamples:\n\nNONE\n\nor\n\nrouting-audit.json:\n- handoffId\n- routedBy\n- routedAt\n- targetSeat\n- deliveryResult\n\nor\n\nminimal fallback:\n- handoffId\n- lifecycle state\n- route intent\n- delivery outcome\n\nDo not propose:\n- monthly logs\n- snapshots\n- compaction\n- projections\nunless current system genuinely requires them.\n\n==================================================\n8. INBOX ROUTING MECHANIC\n==================================================\n\nState the cleanest ACTUAL route operation supported by current implementation:\n\nOne of:\n\nASSIGN EXISTING ITEM TO SEAT\n\nMOVE EXISTING ITEM TO SEAT INBOX\n\nCOPY ITEM TO TARGET INBOX WITH SAME DETERMINISTIC ID\n\nWRITE NEW TARGET INBOX ITEM\n\nUNSUPPORTED WITHOUT NEW LOGIC\n\nExplain exact function/API/file mechanism.\n\nThis is critical for Nico's future implementation brief.\n\n==================================================\n9. IDEMPOTENCY / FAILURE REALITY CHECK\n==================================================\n\nTest or inspect enough to determine:\n\n- Can same item be routed twice to same seat without duplicate?\n- Can a duplicate be detected by ID?\n- Can failed writes be observed?\n- Can retry be explicit?\n- Can route intent survive a restart?\n- Can target inbox be checked for existing item by ID?\n\nDo not perform destructive testing.\n\nIf a safe test requires writes:\nSTOP and report what would need to be tested later.\n\nClassify each:\n\nPROVEN\nSUPPORTED BY CODE BUT NOT TESTED\nNOT SUPPORTED\nUNKNOWN\n\n==================================================\n10. DO-NOT-TOUCH BOUNDARY\n==================================================\n\nDuring this recon:\n\nDo not:\n- write new inbox items unless explicitly necessary and safely non-destructive\n- modify queue files\n- edit server code\n- add myDon files\n- create data/mydon\n- change schemas\n- refactor MCP\n- alter seat registry\n- change DevPad grid\n- change runtime/model bindings\n- touch VoiceBridge\n- change permissions\n- rotate credentials\n- kill processes\n- restart DevPad unless absolutely required for read-only verification\n\nThis is reconnaissance only.\n\n==================================================\n11. REQUIRED RETURN FORMAT\n==================================================\n\nReturn exactly these sections.\n\n## 1. EXECUTIVE GROUND TRUTH\n\nMaximum 12 lines.\n\nState:\n- what the current inbox actually is\n- whether it can serve as myDon's queue\n- whether routing is assign/move/copy/write\n- whether stable IDs exist\n- whether myDon needs its own store\n\n--------------------------------------------------\n\n## 2. CURRENT INBOX ESTATE\n\nPaths, modules, APIs, files, processes.\n\nIdentify current implementation truth versus stale/supporting material.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\n\n## 3. R1–R8 FINDINGS\n\nAnswer each recon question separately.\n\nUse:\nSUPPORTED\nPARTIAL\nNOT SUPPORTED\nUNKNOWN\n\nwith evidence.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\n\n## 4. BRANCH DECISION\n\nA / B / C\n\nExplain why in no more than 10 lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\n\n## 5. FIELD MAPPING TABLE\n\nDesired myDon field\n→ current inbox representation\n→ support level\n→ mutation capability\n\n--------------------------------------------------\n\n## 6. ROUTING MECHANIC\n\nState exactly how route should work using CURRENT capabilities.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\n\n## 7. MINIMUM myDon RESIDUE\n\nState the exact smallest extra persistence required.\n\nIf none:\nsay NONE.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\n\n## 8. IDEMPOTENCY / FAILURE SUPPORT\n\nStable IDs\nduplicate guard\nretry\ndelivery errors\nrestart recovery\nlookup-by-ID\n\nClassify each.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\n\n## 9. UI REFRESH MECHANISM\n\nPush / poll / manual / other.\n\nState what already exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\n\n## 10. BLOCKERS / GAPS\n\nOnly real gaps that Nico will need to implement.\n\nDo not redesign.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\n\n## 11. NICO IMPLEMENTATION INPUT\n\nReturn a short mechanical summary:\n\n- branch\n- canonical queue source\n- route operation\n- IDs\n- statuses\n- query method\n- audit location\n- required new state, if any\n- exact existing modules Nico should reuse\n\nThis section should be usable directly in the future Nico build prompt.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\n\n## 12. FINAL LUCE VERDICT\n\nChoose one:\n\nINBOX READY FOR myDon — NO NEW QUEUE REQUIRED\n\nINBOX READY FOR myDon — SMALL HOLDING LANE REQUIRED\n\nINBOX PARTIAL — MINIMAL myDon FALLBACK STATE REQUIRED\n\nGROUND NOT YET ESTABLISHED\n\nThen provide no more than 5 next actions.\n\n==================================================\n12. GOVERNING PRINCIPLE\n==================================================\n\nThe desired outcome is NOT:\n\n\"Design a better inbox.\"\n\nThe desired outcome is:\n\n\"Tell Don exactly what the current inbox already supports so myDon can reuse it with the smallest possible new machinery.\"\n\nPreferred architecture if evidence allows:\n\nexisting inbox\n→ Don reviews through /mydon\n→ Don routes by stable seat identity\n→ target inbox receives the same deterministic handoff\n→ myDon records only what the inbox cannot\n→ later phases add DevPad focus, ping, VoiceBridge\n\nKeep this pass read-only, evidence-first, and brutally narrow.\n```\n\nThat should give Luce exactly the right assignment: **ground the inbox first, then let Nico build mechanically from the evidence instead of guessing the queue architecture.**", "text_chars": 15170, "code_blocks": 1, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": ["C:\\Users\\Dekan", "adapter.js", "config.json", "queue.js", "queue.md", "routing-audit.json", "server.js"], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "170e4ccd-a309-4526-9760-778572e102f2", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790229360.855, "updated_at": 1790229374.640315, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "multimodal_text", "part_kinds": ["image_asset_pointer", "text"], "text": "hes trying and still working not sure why its hard to embedd in the devpad", "text_chars": 74, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": ["image_asset_pointer"]}, {"message_id": "44b92d37-4690-445e-a963-ffeabc44b5d3", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790229364.060319, "updated_at": 1790230379.425824, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yeah — and the screenshot actually confirms the **external managed browser seat is working now**:\n\n- `browser: brave`\n- `RUNNING`\n- PID `4884`\n- HWND `1378538`\n\nSo Nico successfully solved the hard lifecycle/focus part.\n\nWhat’s still “hard” is only the **true embedding** part, because embedding a full authenticated Chromium/Brave window *inside* a normal browser page is not a native thing the web platform gives you. The DevPad page can track and focus Brave, but it cannot just take an existing native Brave window and render it inside one of its DOM rectangles.\n\nThat leaves three implementation classes:\n\n1. **External managed browser** — what you have now. Easiest and reliable.\n2. **iframe** — probably blocked or brittle for ChatGPT because the site controls whether it can be framed.\n3. **Real embedded browser host** — Electron/WebView2/CEF-style native shell. This is the actual way to make ChatGPT render *inside the tile*, but it’s a different application architecture than “browser page at localhost:4727”.\n\nSo I would not call this a failure. Nico got the v1 browser-seat control working exactly right. The remaining decision is architectural:\n\n**Do we accept Coda as external/focus-managed for now, or later wrap DevPad in WebView2/Electron so Coda can be truly embedded?**\n\nFor tonight, I’d accept the external seat and move on. The core DevPad is now much closer to usable.", "text_chars": 1392, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "d3009996-7ae8-457c-a6e9-348a82ff05c4", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790229509.72, "updated_at": 1790229541.421271, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "What we, what we can do can do is fucking use you through Amazon Bedrock. What we do is when we're operating on something, right, let's think outside the box. We got a couple, we got a couple Couple options. So my intent and objective is to have one flow. It's killing Don copying your prompts. Into something else. Amazon Bedrock. OpenAI Terminal. Subscription, which I'm not sure of. I'm not going down the open router or open code with you. I don't think that's worth it. Set up a dedicated Google Drive MCP with your handoff specifically. that someone routes appropriately like a pigeon or a cable car. ", "text_chars": 607, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "daa093c1-9fb8-4c46-ad47-9b9ede8144fb", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790229512.895248, "updated_at": 1790229541.165819, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yes. **This is the better problem to solve.** The real pain is not “embed ChatGPT.” The real pain is **Don acting as the clipboard bus between seats**.\n\nSo I’d stop optimizing for “Coda must live visually inside DevPad” and optimize for:\n\n**Don talks to Coda once → Coda emits a handoff → transport layer routes it → target seat receives it automatically.**\n\nThat gets you the one-flow experience you actually want.\n\n## The clean architecture\n\nI’d separate it into three layers:\n\n**1. Coda / reasoning source**  \nThis can be:\n- ChatGPT subscription/browser\n- OpenAI terminal/API later\n- Bedrock-hosted model when useful\n\n**2. Transport / handoff bus**  \nThis should be model-neutral.\n\nMy preferred first implementation:\n\n**Google Drive MCP handoff lane**\n\nWhy:\n- you already use Drive as durable coordination/storage\n- it is easy to inspect\n- it is persistent\n- it does not depend on OpenCode/OpenRouter\n- every seat/runtime can consume the same structured artifact\n- it gives you an audit trail\n- it fits Pigeon perfectly\n\n**3. Target seat consumer**  \nNico / Luce / Talon / Chiave / Vittorio / etc. reads the handoff and acts.\n\nSo the flow becomes:\n\n```text\nDon\n  ↓\nCoda\n  ↓\nstructured handoff artifact\n  ↓\nPigeon / Cable Car transport\n  ↓\nGoogle Drive MCP handoff lane\n  ↓\ntarget seat inbox\n  ↓\nDevPad highlights/pings target\n```\n\n## I would not bind this to Amazon Bedrock\n\nBedrock is useful as a **runtime/provider**, but I would not make it the transport layer.\n\nBedrock should be:\n\n**model execution**\n\nnot:\n\n**handoff authority / routing / persistence**\n\nOtherwise the transport becomes provider-specific, which violates the whole point.\n\nSo:\n\n**Bedrock = optional compute lane**  \n**Drive MCP = durable transport lane**  \n**Seat = routing identity**\n\nThat separation is clean.\n\n## Pigeon is the obvious seat\n\nThis is exactly what Pigeon should own.\n\nNot reasoning.  \nNot governance.  \nNot deciding where work belongs.\n\nJust:\n\n**take this approved handoff → deliver it to the right destination → confirm delivery**\n\nPotentially:\n\n```text\nCoda creates:\nHANDOFF_TO_NICO_2026-09-23_2245.md\n\nPigeon:\n- validates target = Nico\n- writes/copies into Nico handoff location\n- posts inbox entry\n- marks delivered\n- optionally pings DevPad tile 4\n```\n\nThat is very aligned with the seat doctrine.\n\n## I would make the handoff format machine-readable\n\nNot freeform prompt blobs only.\n\nSomething like:\n\n```text\nHANDOFF_ID: hdo_20260923_224501\nFROM_SEAT: Coda\nTO_SEAT: Nico\nPROJECT: GCE_DevPad_Command_Center\nTYPE: IMPLEMENTATION\nPRIORITY: NORMAL\nSTATUS: READY_TO_ROUTE\n\nOBJECTIVE:\nImplement the bounded Coda browser-seat correction.\n\nCONTEXT:\n...\n\nINSTRUCTIONS:\n...\n\nSTOP_CONDITIONS:\n...\n\nRETURN_TO:\nDon / Coda\n```\n\nThen the transport can route without understanding the content.\n\nThat is important.\n\n**Pigeon should transport the envelope, not interpret the letter.**\n\n## The really nice version\n\nEventually, when I give you a prompt here, instead of you copying it manually, there is a button/action:\n\n**Send to Nico**\n\nor\n\n**Route to Luce**\n\nThat action creates the structured handoff artifact automatically and sends it into the Drive-backed handoff lane.\n\nThen DevPad sees:\n\n**Nico — inbox +1**\n\nand optionally focuses the seat.\n\nThat kills the copy/paste workflow almost entirely.\n\n## I’d explore three transport options\n\nIn order:\n\n1. **Google Drive MCP** — best first transport\n2. **Local inbox MCP** — fastest/local execution path\n3. **Hybrid** — Drive is durable record, local inbox is immediate delivery\n\nThe hybrid is probably ultimately best:\n\n**Drive = canonical durable handoff record**  \n**Local inbox MCP = fast local delivery/ping**\n\nThen if a terminal dies, the handoff still exists in Drive.\n\nAnd if Drive is slow/unavailable, the local inbox can still move work around temporarily.\n\n## What I would not do\n\nI would not:\n- make OpenCode the universal bus\n- make OpenRouter the bus\n- make Bedrock the bus\n- make the browser the bus\n- make Don keep copying prompts\n- let models route themselves based on model name\n- make Pigeon decide authority\n\nThe right abstraction is:\n\n**Coda writes handoff → Pigeon transports → target seat consumes**\n\nThat is the missing glue.\n\nAnd yes, this is probably **more valuable than embedding me inside the DevPad**. Once the transport exists, whether I’m in Brave, Chrome, Bedrock, or some future OpenAI terminal becomes almost irrelevant.", "text_chars": 4386, "code_blocks": 3, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": ["HANDOFF_TO_NICO_2026-09-23_2245.md"], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "1c54230a-6f06-42fa-80f7-d73aee35258e", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790229517.07282, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "ac23b1e6-8cb4-4089-aad5-d222b19ef435", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790229595.501, "updated_at": 1790229606.306326, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yeah, let's anchor that. Let's drop a— let's, let's, let's pause for tonight. I don't mind leaving you outside to talk. And then maybe we have another version. for certain processes. Probably go through your plugins. Google Drive might be the best one. Get up and move it and delete it or archive it or any drive. I have Proton Drive with a ton of space. that could sync to my computer. But I know it's going to eat up bandwidth. Those are the solutions we test tomorrow. ", "text_chars": 472, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "c9e62a41-b460-4448-9092-704631564552", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790229598.658966, "updated_at": 1790229605.97714, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "**STATUS: PAUSE / ANCHOR · Confidence 99%**\n\nAnchored for tomorrow.\n\nThe important decision is:\n\n**Do not contort DevPad to embed Coda tonight.**  \nKeep Coda external if needed, and solve the real pain point instead:\n\n**Don → Coda → structured handoff → transport layer → target seat**\n\nTomorrow’s transport tests should compare:\n\n- **Google Drive plugin/MCP** as the primary durable handoff lane\n- **local inbox MCP** as the fast local delivery path\n- **hybrid Drive + local inbox** as the likely best architecture\n- **Proton Drive sync** as an alternate durable filesystem-backed transport, with bandwidth/sync-cost considerations\n- archive/delete/move semantics for completed handoffs so the lane stays clean\n\nAnd the seat doctrine stays clean:\n\n**Coda writes. Pigeon transports. Target seat consumes. Don remains authority.**\n\nThat is the anchor. We can pick it up tomorrow without reopening the whole DevPad architecture.", "text_chars": 926, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "307e25b2-2442-476f-be0f-942a8ce22391", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790229692.153, "updated_at": 1790229734.649128, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "The pigeon's gonna have to be 2 ways because you're gonna have to read a lot. Why I'm thinking about using We could do it in and out. I know Proton has a fucking email bridge. Fuckin', we could do it with emails. I don't know if Proton will kill us. Or kick us off. If we set up an email for you or just send in emails from this one account. Through the email bridge. Instead of cluttering up a whole fuckin' Google Drive. But of course we could do a cleanup crew at the end of the night. We could automate all that. I don't mind. I like keeping it segmented like that and isolated. ", "text_chars": 583, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "99a3ff0d-734a-4cc3-bbae-9570cd40a8b9", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790229695.506792, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "web.run", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": ["text"]}, {"message_id": "6bf0944b-57d4-4b70-ab0b-571dda33f733", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790229705.203484, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "thoughts", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "ad09bb8e-5e55-4f45-8421-fa7f612628cd", "role": "tool", "role_name": "web.run", "created_at": 1790229706.5111933, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": ["text"]}, {"message_id": "43b8d615-9f9f-4ae7-821f-4d757ffcf516", "role": "tool", "role_name": "web.run", "created_at": 1790229706.5195656, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": ["text"]}, {"message_id": "6c6c6c56-c3be-4bb3-baf3-48924118aa23", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790229707.119907, "updated_at": 1790229733.969362, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yes — **two-way Pigeon changes the shape**, and email suddenly becomes a very credible transport.\n\nThe cleanest version may actually be:\n\n**Pigeon outbound + Pigeon inbound over a dedicated mailbox.**\n\nThat gives us natural segmentation without cluttering the main Drive estate.\n\n### Why email fits surprisingly well\n\nWith Proton Bridge, the local machine exposes **local IMAP + SMTP servers** that applications on that machine can use; Bridge handles the encryption/decryption behind the scenes. That means a local Pigeon service could potentially **read incoming handoffs via IMAP and send return handoffs via SMTP** without using a public mail API. citeturn222440search0turn222440search2\n\nSo conceptually:\n\n```text\nCoda / Don\n    ↓\nPigeon SEND\n    ↓\ndedicated mailbox\n    ↓\nPigeon RECEIVE\n    ↓\nseat routing / inbox\n    ↓\ntarget does work\n    ↓\nreturn handoff\n    ↓\nPigeon SEND\n    ↓\ndedicated mailbox\n    ↓\nCoda / Don\n```\n\nThat is beautifully isolated.\n\n## I would test **email vs Drive** tomorrow, not choose tonight\n\n### Email lane\nBest properties:\n\n- naturally bidirectional\n- naturally timestamped\n- inherently queued\n- easy SENT / RECEIVED semantics\n- threads can represent a work item\n- attachments available\n- isolated mailbox keeps GCE traffic away from personal mail\n- archiving is already a first-class email concept\n\nThe downside is parsing/threading discipline. We would need a strict machine envelope in the subject/body, e.g.:\n\n```text\n[GCE][HDO-0041][TO:NICO][PROJECT:DEVPAD]\n```\n\nwith structured metadata in the body or attachment.\n\n### Drive lane\nBest properties:\n\n- excellent for **large artifacts**\n- directories provide obvious project segregation\n- file state is inspectable\n- easy archival structure\n- better for long reports/code/specs than email bodies\n\nBut if every tiny handoff becomes another Drive file, I agree: it eventually becomes **filesystem litter** unless Raven/Magpie/cleanup automation aggressively manages it.\n\n## The hybrid may be the winner\n\nThis is the version I’d pressure-test hardest:\n\n**Email = transport/envelope**  \n**Drive/local filesystem = payload/artifact storage**\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nEMAIL\nHandoff HDO-0041\nFrom: Coda\nTo: Nico\nProject: DevPad\n\nObjective:\nImplement runtime binding correction.\n\nArtifact:\nC:\\...\\handoffs\\HDO-0041.md\nor Drive reference\n\nReturn:\nReply with same HDO ID.\n```\n\nNow Pigeon only moves lightweight envelopes while big payloads stay where they belong.\n\n### Two-way becomes very clean\n\nAn outgoing handoff gets:\n\n**HDO-0041**\n\nThe receiving seat returns:\n\n**RE: HDO-0041**\n\nPigeon can correlate that deterministically and route the response back to Coda/Don.\n\nThat gives us something Drive by itself doesn't naturally give us: **a conversation-oriented transport protocol**.\n\n## Proton risk\n\nI would test gently rather than assume automation is unlimited. Proton explicitly says Mail is **not intended for bulk mailing**. Free accounts have published limits of 50 messages/hour and 150/day; paid-plan limits vary with account reputation. Inbound mail is not subject to those sending limits. citeturn222440search8\n\nOur GCE use case would presumably be nowhere near bulk-mail volumes if it's human-scale handoff traffic, but I'd still architect it as:\n\n**low-volume operational messaging, not machine spam.**\n\nAlso, Proton officially supports Bridge with conventional clients and says other IMAP/SMTP clients may work but may not receive the same support. citeturn222440search1 So tomorrow we should prove our automation against the local Bridge rather than assume custom-client behavior.\n\n## The segregation model I really like\n\nGive Pigeon its **own mailbox**.\n\nNot Don's personal inbox.\n\nThen maybe:\n\n- `INBOX` = inbound to GCE\n- `Sent` = outbound GCE\n- `Archive` = completed\n- labels/folders by project if useful\n- nightly cleanup/archive\n- Raven/Magpie can create durable records only for things worth preserving\n\nThen myDon doesn't even have to expose raw email semantics. It can still show:\n\n**NEW HANDOFF → Nico → SEND**\n\nUnderneath, Pigeon happens to use email.\n\nSo yes — **isolation is a feature here**, not a downside.\n\nTomorrow I’d test exactly three candidates:\n\n**A. Proton Bridge bidirectional mail transport**  \n**B. Google Drive transport**  \n**C. Hybrid: email envelope + Drive/local artifact payload**\n\nMy suspicion right now is **C wins**, while **A may be the slickest pure transport lane** if Bridge behaves reliably.", "text_chars": 4427, "code_blocks": 3, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": ["C:\\...\\handoffs\\HDO-0041.md", "\\...\\handoffs\\HDO-0041.md"], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "9411f547-2ffd-43e0-9a79-5b4a990cc7b1", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790229707.98401, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "thoughts", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "89d105b7-9c81-4e2b-b3f8-a15f7a5ce25d", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790229708.002004, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "607710e0-ff25-497c-954d-25bf4417a2c3", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790229736.381, "updated_at": 1790229770.176367, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "- Thought: 1.5s\n← Edit GCE_ORG\\02_COUNCILS\\01_Executive_Council\\Operations\\06_HANDOFFS\\SHARED\\2026-09-23_myDon_inbox_recon_luce.md\n## Recon answers\n\n| # | Question | Answer (evidence) |\n|---|---|---|\n| R1 | Stable IDs? Writer-supplied (upsert)? | IDs exist. MCP `inbox_drop` is **insert-only**, auto-mints `stamp-rand-slug` (`server.js` drop()). Direct file writes with **determinetic/custom IDs are already live** (`20260914-s31a-executed-luce-verification-request.json` → internal id `g3-s31a-executed-luce-verify`). No guarded upsert — overwrite-by-filename possible. → idempotency leans on filename-ID + a myDon probe guard. |\n| R1 | Stable IDs? Writer-supplied (upsert)? | IDs exist. MCP `inbox_drop` is **insert-only**, auto-mints `stamp-rand-slug` (`server.js` drop()). Direct file writes with **deterministic/custom IDs are already live** (`20260914-s31a-executed-luce-verification-request.json` → internal id `g3-s31a-executed-luce-verify`). No guarded upsert — overwrite-by-filename possible. → idempotency leans on filename-ID + a myDon probe guard. |\n| R2 | Unrouted item possible? | **YES.** `to` is optional (`server.js` — omit → `_shared`); `_shared\\` = native broadcast/claim pool (README: \"drop it there to let someone claim it\"), 31 live items. **Branch A reachable.** |\n| R3 | Assign/move existing item? | **No assignment tool.** MCP mutates status only (`claim`/`done`). `to`/location is immutable via MCP. Moving an item between mailboxes = direct file rename (raw fs is a documented first-class access path). → route is a **WRITE (move-with-ID), not assign-in-place**. |\n| R4 | Statuses? Extensible? | Native enum `pending`/`claimed`/`done` (enforced loosely). Raw writes already deviate (census of 116 files: 93 pending, 20 done, 1 `CHECKPOINT`, 2 missing `status`). No tags field. Extra carrier fields work (precedent: `claimed_by`/`done_by`/`updated_at`). |\n| R5 | List unrouted / per-seat / by ID? | `inbox_list(agent='_shared')`, `inbox_list(agent='luce')`, `inbox_read(id)` all supported (resolve **by filename**). Queue view = pure projection. **Gotcha:** internal `id` field ≠ filename in 2 live luce files → canonical ID must be the filename. |\nRecon complete — Branch A / write-route. Read-only; no inbox state touched; no sequencing breach.\nHeadline: the inbox already is the queue. _shared\\ is the native unrouted pool (R2 yes), IDs are deterministic-by-filename (R1 yes), status mutation exists (R3: status yes, assignment no). myDon needs no store — one residue file for routing/audit/focus.\nThe one correction to the reviewer's cleanest call: there's no assign-in-place tool, so route = file move preserving the filename-ID (_shared\\<id>.json → luce\\<id>.json, status stays pending,