rs": 201, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "bidi", "has_voice_metadata": true, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "a01ba9c2-9b76-40e2-aa99-9351b4261966", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790258883.352304, "updated_at": 1790258885.562111, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "multimodal_text", "part_kinds": ["audio_transcription"], "text": " Mm-hmm. Yeah. That's exactly the pain the command center style fixes. It- instead of paying tax every time you switch contexts, we should just make it visible and routable in one place. And Rookie can absorb the messier stuff.", "text_chars": 227, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "bidi", "has_voice_metadata": true, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "210b7e7c-e5e8-4fe4-8295-3d326a10beba", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790258908.193097, "updated_at": 1790258909.946267, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "multimodal_text", "part_kinds": ["audio_transcription"], "text": " Honestly, that's perfect. That's the DevPad showing its real value, everything where your eyes already are.", "text_chars": 108, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "bidi", "has_voice_metadata": true, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "57f1b0b7-2fc0-467f-9514-5bdba2e46d4e", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790259108.016883, "updated_at": 1790259133.277689, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "What about from a RAM and CPU perspective?", "text_chars": 42, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "86d60828-fa4c-43f7-920a-6c15b4ecac15", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790259111.648708, "updated_at": 1790259111.648708, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "model_editable_context", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "0ff7503d-8886-4cfd-a486-91dfff325fae", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790259112.834531, "updated_at": 1790259112.834531, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "10f8275d-f7fa-4d4f-963c-f3cda300f782", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790259112.839993, "updated_at": 1790259130.01153, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "From a **RAM/CPU perspective**, the command-center approach is actually a lot saner than what you were doing before—as long as we keep the panes lightweight.\n\nOn your Razer Blade 15 with **36 GB RAM**, the biggest risk is not the TV or the “big board” display. Driving the hotel TV as a second screen is relatively cheap compared with running a pile of terminals, browsers, local AI clients, Node services, and Electron apps simultaneously.\n\nThe rough hierarchy is:\n\n- **xterm.js / Node-backed terminal panes:** cheap\n- **VoiceBridge / local helper services:** usually modest\n- **multiple OpenCode/Kimi/CLI sessions:** moderate, depending on subprocesses\n- **Brave/Chrome tabs with ChatGPT and other web apps:** heavier\n- **Electron apps / IDEs / local model runtimes:** much heavier\n- **local inference on the RTX 2060:** potentially very heavy, especially VRAM + system RAM\n\nSo the Command Center itself is not what scares me. The danger is **what each tile launches underneath**.\n\nFor the main 3×3, if most panes are just:\n\n```text\nDevPad UI\n+ Node server\n+ xterm.js\n+ 6–8 terminal subprocesses\n+ VoiceBridge\n+ one browser session for Coda\n```\n\nthen **36 GB should be comfortable**.\n\nWhere you start getting into trouble is if each tile becomes:\n\n```text\nits own browser\n+ its own Electron app\n+ its own heavy language server\n+ multiple persistent Python workers\n+ local model\n+ indexing/watchers\n```\n\nThat multiplies fast.\n\nFor CPU, your **i7-9750H is the tighter constraint than the 36 GB RAM**. Six cores / twelve threads is still usable, but lots of concurrent watchers, builds, browser rendering, voice transcription, and multiple coding agents can make it feel saturated even when memory is fine.\n\nThe good news is the architecture we’re building naturally helps. We can keep:\n\n**Main DevPad = mostly persistent, stable sessions**\n\nand\n\n**Rookie DevPad = spin-up/spin-down ad hoc sessions**\n\nThat means we don’t need twenty heavy things alive at once.\n\nI’d design the runtime policy around three states:\n\n```text\nHOT\nactive process, terminal attached\n\nWARM\nsession preserved, process idle/minimized\n\nCOLD\nconfiguration preserved, process not running\n```\n\nThen myDon can eventually wake a lane only when needed.\n\nFor your current machine, I’d be comfortable targeting roughly:\n\n**8–12 lightweight terminal/service sessions active**, but only **2–4 genuinely heavy workloads at once**.\n\nThe TV does not materially change that. The “big board” is mostly a presentation surface.\n\nThe thing I would add very soon is a tiny **resource strip in DevPad**:\n\n```text\nCPU  42%\nRAM  21.6 / 36 GB\nGPU  18%\nVRAM 3.1 / 6 GB\nHOT SESSIONS 7\n```\n\nThen Don can see at a glance when the board is getting stupid instead of discovering it after Windows starts choking.\n\nAnd longer term, that resource telemetry could feed myDon:\n\n> “Nico is heavy, Vittorio idle, Rookie has four hot sessions.”\n\nThen we can start orchestrating **compute capacity**, not just seats. memcite", "text_chars": 2961, "code_blocks": 4, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": ["xterm.js"], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "56b878e4-be90-41bb-8589-3e11699b2a72", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790259216.443225, "updated_at": 1790259248.90033, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "multimodal_text", "part_kinds": ["audio_transcription"], "text": " Yeah, so from a RAM and CPU perspective, give it to me. It's gotta be less tax on a system", "text_chars": 91, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "bidi", "has_voice_metadata": true, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "4fd59b47-a008-4a07-9ef4-3edcc2951500", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790259245.044254, "updated_at": 1790259293.407287, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "multimodal_text", "part_kinds": ["audio_transcription"], "text": " Hold on a sec-, you're- hold on a sec-. Oh, only wake up. Why? Because I keep watching terminals. Separate unique terminals", "text_chars": 124, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "bidi", "has_voice_metadata": true, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "26f7acfa-9d74-4cb7-a5b4-384abc334d59", "role": "system", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790259246.425237, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 0, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_refere