l", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "19. FINAL POSTMORTEM\nDon asked for a copy/paste fix and a seat swap. He got the seat swap — clean, verified, live — and a working keyboard clipboard. He also got a silent, unrecorded withdrawal of one required behavior, a shipped build that fails the acceptance criteria written for it, three gate runs whose target build is permanently unknowable, one unrecoverable build generation, and 9½ hours in which nothing visible to him changed.\nThe loop was not caused by orchestration drift. Coda was not in the room. It was generated by a two-party verification cycle with no stop condition, in which the failing object's definition was allowed to change every round. Each round produced: a new theory, a new build, a new test shape — and zero closures. The mechanism moved mousedown → onSelectionChange → mousemove → deleted in three hours. What finally stopped it was not a decision; it was a source-code comment at 22:36:30 that nobody wrote a handoff for.\nThe most consequential single fact is not in the clipboard thread at all. It is that renderer.js was verified with harnesses that could not prove what they were testing — no reload, no cache pin, no version marker, no server cache headers, and one harness that hard-coded a specific build's hash as its own expected value. For three runs the evidence is not weak; it is unrecoverable. And there is no git and no backup, so the intermediate builds exist only as files in %TEMP% that a cleanup will delete.\nThe circuit breaker that was missing was not technical. It was the sentence: \"seven of eight rows work. Ship that, and decide separately about the eighth.\"\nReward the stop, not the build. That stop was available at 16:46 and it was never taken.\nFINAL FOOTER\nSESSION STATUS:        LOOP-CONTAMINATED\n\nORIGINAL ASK:          (1) Fix copy/paste in the DevPad terminal.\n                        (2) Replace Seat 1's browser/Coda surface with a normal\n                   