    sound; 3 are permanently indeterminate; 1 was never gated.\nEVIDENCE GAP:         No DevPad server log for 09-23/24. No gate result JSON (all gates\n                      exit 0 on FAIL). No git, no backups. Builds at 22:05:31 and 22:36:30\n                      have no artifact and no named author. The referenced Coda ruling text\n                      does not exist on disk. Gate verdicts from 16:57-17:06 are\n                      reconstructed from handoff prose, not from gate stdout.\nNEXT VERIFICATION GATE: None. This is an autopsy. Any forward motion is a build decision\n                      and belongs to Nico under Don's authority, not to Luce.", "text_chars": 8486, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [".server.log", "GCE_DON\\AGENTS.md", "config.json", "luce_d2_behavioral\\harness.js", "nico_d2_sim.js", "nico_d2_static_validate.js", "notes.md", "renderer.js"], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "cd35de11-d6f7-4954-b951-6d108656ab4c", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790365905.345992, "updated_at": 1790365905.345992, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "model_editable_context", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "f38a9ed5-0f1d-4bd7-b952-ab9f4f01c6a8", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790365911.301282, "updated_at": 1790365911.301282, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "a07a5644-73af-4833-97fb-0dbbcd15cbe0", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790365911.30582, "updated_at": 1790365951.553855, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yes — **this materially changes the postmortem**, and we should correct our earlier narrative rather than defend it.\n\nThe biggest correction is this:\n\n> **The loop was not primarily Coda orchestration drift inside that runtime thread. According to Space Bunny/Luce’s forensic reconstruction, Coda, Chiave, and Vittorio produced zero artifacts in the actual clipboard loop. The recursion was occurring directly inside the Nico ↔ Luce verification/build pair.**\n\nThat matters. Earlier, I attributed too much causal responsibility to Coda based on the conversational pattern we were seeing here. The filesystem/handoff reconstruction says the actual execution chain was different.\n\n## What Space Bunny appears to have established\n\n### 1. The loop point is much more precise than we had\n\nThe strongest circuit-breaker was apparently available at **16:46:39**:\n\n- D-1 was green.\n- **7 of 8 rows were working.**\n- Seat 1 worked.\n- Keyboard copy/paste worked.\n- Only right-click copy remained unresolved.\n\nThat was the moment the work should have been classified and closed or explicitly escalated.\n\nInstead, the definition of the remaining defect started mutating:\n\n**mousedown**\n→ `onSelectionChange`\n→ `mousemove`\n→ eventually **deleted/deferred**\n\nThat is a much sharper description of the loop than “clipboard debugging took too long.”\n\nThe object under test itself kept changing.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Nico was implementing unproven runtime assumptions\n\nThis recon identifies specific assumptions with timestamps:\n\n- `mousedown` snapshot is early enough\n- `onSelectionChange` fires early enough\n- static simulation adequately represents live runtime behavior\n\nThose weren't just hypotheses discussed casually. They became the basis for successive builds **before the corresponding runtime behavior had been established**.\n\nThat supports the concern you raised yesterday:\n\n> Somebody was guessing at the architecture/event behavior.\n\nYes.\n\nAccording to this recon, the principal guesses entered through the **implementation cycle**, particularly Nico's event-model assumptions.\n\nThat does **not** mean Nico acted deceptively. It means the engineering sequence was backwards:\n\n**implementation assumption → build → verifier disproves**\n\ninstead of:\n\n**runtime observation → build once**\n\n---\n\n### 3. Luce verification itself became recursive\n\nThis report also doesn't simply absolve Luce.\n\nThe key phrase is:\n\n> **“two-party verification cycle with no stop condition”**\n\nThat's different from saying Luce found fake defects.\n\nThe defects were mostly real.\n\nThe failure was that each FAIL:\n\n1. redefined the defect,\n2. produced a new theory,\n3. produced another build,\n4. produced a different test shape,\n5. generated another FAIL,\n6. never forced a ship/defer decision.\n\nSo Luce was doing verification work, but the **Nico↔Luce relationship had no circuit breaker**.\n\nThat is a framework issue we need to retain.\n\n---\n\n### 4. The verification evidence itself was weaker than we believed\n\nThis may be the most serious technical finding.\n\nAccording to the recon, multiple gates could not establish **which renderer build was actually executing** because there was:\n\n- no reload guarantee\n- no renderer version marker\n- no cache pin\n- no relevant cache headers\n- no durable gate-result artifact tying execution to a build\n- no git/history\n- no durable backup chain\n\nAnd one harness apparently hard-coded a particular expected hash.\n\nIf this is correct, then some things we previously called “verified” were not merely imperfectly verified.\n\nThey are now classified as:\n\n> **permanently indeterminate**\n\nThat means we must be careful not to use those three gate runs as proof in tomorrow's reconciliation.\n\n---\n\n### 5. The final behavior was not “fixed”; it was silently withdrawn\n\nThis is another major correction.\n\nThe final state wasn't:\n\n> right-click copy eventually fixed.\n\nIt appears to have become:\n\n> **right-click copy removed/deferred through a source-code change/comment without a corresponding handoff or Don ruling.**\n\nThat is a governance problem separate from the bug.\n\nThe implementation apparently changed the acceptance surface without an explicit decision record.\n\nThat is important because it explains why yesterday felt so nonsensical:\n\n**the session spent hours trying to satisfy an acceptance criterion and then ultimately stopped satisfying it by changing the criterion.**\n\nThat's not closure.\n\n---\n\n# The revised forensic picture\n\nI would now frame the blackhole like this:\n\n**Original state**\n\nWorking DevPad + broken clipboard behavior + Seat 1 browser surface.\n\n↓\n\n**Useful work**\n\nSeat 1 successfully converted.  \nKeyboard clipboard behavior brought to 7/8 green.\n\n↓\n\n**Circuit breaker missed**\n\nAt ~16:46, a usable bounded result existed.\n\n↓\n\n**Loop**\n\nNico and Luce entered recursive implementation/verification around one remaining row.\n\n↓\n\n**Defect definition mutates**\n\nmousedown → selection event → mousemove → further probes.\n\n↓\n\n**Evidence quality deteriorates**\n\nSome gates cannot be tied conclusively to specific builds.\n\n↓\n\n**No explicit product decision**\n\nNobody says:\n\n> Ship 7/8 and defer row 4.\n\n↓\n\n**Implicit withdrawal**\n\nThe stubborn behavior is apparently removed/deferred in code without a proper decision artifact.\n\n↓\n\n**10+ hours elapsed**\n\nNo corresponding visible feature development.\n\nThat reconstruction fits the evidence Space Bunny reports **better than my earlier Coda-centric explanation**.\n\n## One point I would not yet promote to canon\n\nThere are several striking claims in the report that I would preserve as **Luce forensic findings**, not yet silently turn into permanent framework truth:\n\n- exactly **10h 59m 13s**\n- exactly **44% of the day** going to ECQ\n- the live-freeze/override conflict around the Coda pane\n- two separate codebases both being called DevPad\n- authorship of build generations 3 and 4 being unknown\n\nThose are highly relevant, but they should remain attributed to this recon until we cross-reconcile the other contaminated sessions.\n\n## Most important correction to our framework postmortem\n\nYesterday I said:\n\n> Coda repeatedly turned Luce findings into new slices.\n\nBased on this forensic report, **that statement is not supported for the actual execution thread**.\n\nThe stronger evidence now says:\n\n> **The loop could sustain itself locally between Nico and Luce because no enclosing session controller imposed a stop condition, retry ceiling, visible-outcome checkpoint, or decision gate.**\n\nThat's subtler and more important.\n\nCoda's failure may therefore have been **absence of active framing**, rather than **active repeated reframing**.\n\nThose are very different failure modes.\n\nAnd that fits your original concern about the missing session header/footer extremely well.\n\n### Current reconciliation status\n\n**STATUS:** CHECKPOINT  \n**CONFIDENCE:** 94% on the interpretation of the supplied Luce recon; individual filesystem claims remain attributed to Luce until cross-session reconciliation  \n**OUTCOME:** Previous Coda-centric root-cause theory corrected; strongest current mechanism is an unbounded Nico↔Luce build/verification recursion operating without an enclosing session stop condition  \n**KEY NEW FINDING:** 7/8 behavior was available at approximately 16:46; no explicit ship/defer decision fired  \n**CRITICAL EVIDENCE ISSUE:** several later gates reportedly cannot be tied conclusively to the renderer generation under test  \n**NEXT ROUTE:** Don + Coda, continuing the autopsy one item at a time  \n**ANCHOR STATUS:** UPDATED — Space Bunny/Luce forensic report is now the strongest detailed reconstruction we have for this DevPad blackhole.", "text_chars": 7630, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "65dbe2e1-2c7b-4545-a7b2-9b563983a2b8", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790366001.535, "updated_at": 1790366037.767643, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Okay, so outline next steps here as I'm being hands-on today.", "text_chars": 61, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "60d72904-6762-4bf6-8270-b42a0ba20012", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790366010.900309, "updated_at": 1790366010.900309, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "7f3bd1a3-2902-4c1d-9f65-4a67c1a9b895", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790366010.903112, "updated_at": 1790366037.317119, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yes. Given the autopsy, today should be **controlled recovery**, not another “fix everything” session.\n\n## Today’s order of operations\n\n### 1. Freeze the scene first\nBefore touching DevPad code, preserve the evidence that can disappear.\n\nMost important:\n- the `%TEMP%` renderer snapshots\n- the sound harnesses / PNGs\n- the Nico/Luce handoffs\n- current `renderer.js`\n- current `config.json`\n- current session/header/footer artifacts\n- anything tying hashes to build generations\n\nReason: Space Bunny found that some intermediate builds exist only in temp storage. If Windows cleanup deletes them, part of the history is gone.\n\n**Don action:** no cleanup, no “optimize temp,” no rebuild yet.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Establish one authoritative session frame\nBefore anyone launches Nico/Luce again, create one explicit session header that says:\n\n- exact work item\n- exact starting state\n- exact expected visible artifact\n- what is out of scope\n- stop condition\n- maximum retry count\n- visible-outcome checkpoint\n- who owns build\n- who owns verification\n\nThe missing enclosing frame appears to be one of the strongest contributors to the blackhole.\n\nFor today, I would make the operating rule:\n\n> **No seat may create a new slice merely because another seat found a defect. New slices require Don/Coda approval.**\n\n---\n\n### 3. Reconstruct current live DevPad state\nNot history. **Current reality.**\n\nOne read-only recon should answer:\n\n- Which `renderer.js` is on disk?\n- Which behavior is actually in that file?\n- Is right-click copy currently removed/deferred?\n- Is right-click paste changed?\n- What exactly is Seat 1 now?\n- What does the actual browser load today?\n- Is keyboard copy/paste working in the current live board?\n- Which build generation is currently served?\n\nThis should be a **single current-state snapshot**, not another debugging exercise.\n\nThe output should be something like:\n\n**CURRENT LIVE STATE — VERIFIED AS OF NOW**\n\nNo theories.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Don makes the two governance decisions\nThe autopsy surfaced two things that should not be left implicit.\n\n**Decision A — right-click copy**\n\nChoose explicitly:\n\n- required behavior\n- or deferred/non-blocking behavior\n\nYesterday apparently ended with that requirement silently disappearing. We should never allow that again.\n\n**Decision B — Seat 1 freeze/override issue**\n\nSpace B